const EXPLANATIONS = {
"1": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute otitis media is an infection of the middle ear and usually spreads to this space via the eustachian tubes from the nasopharynx. It presents with ear pain, often with fever, an erythematous tympanic membrane with retrotympanic pus or a middle ear effusion, and conductive hearing loss. Commonly implicated pathogens include Streptococcus pneumoniae, nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae, and Moraxella catarrhalis. Treatment is with antibiotics, typically amoxicillin.</p><p>Acute otitis media is an infection of the middle ear that classically occurs in children. Causative agents include viral or bacterial pathogens, commonly Streptococcus pneumoniae, Moraxella catarrhalis, or nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae. It presents with ear pain, often with fever, an erythematous tympanic membrane with retrotympanic pus or a middle ear effusion, and conductive hearing loss. The middle ear is bounded externally by the tympanic membrane and internally by the oval window. It is connected to the nasopharynx via the eustachian (auditory) tubes, which open into the posterior nasopharynx near the torus tubarius. Infection usually spreads to the middle ear by way of the eustachian tubes. First-line therapy includes antibiotic treatment with amoxicillin with or without clavulanate, as well as symptomatic treatment. Recurrent episodes may lead to inflammation of the adjacent mastoid bone, a condition known as mastoiditis. Features of mastoiditis include those of otitis media, plus opacification of mastoid air cells on CT scan and deviation of the ear. Children with recurrent episodes of acute otitis media may benefit from surgical drainage with myringotomy and tympanostomy tube placement.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Bullous otitis (Choice B) is characterized by otalgia, which can be severe, and serous bullae or vesicles of the tympanic membrane, often with fever. Causative agents are most often viral, but Streptococcus pneumoniae and Moraxella catarrhalis are also implicated. First-line therapy includes antibiotic treatment with amoxicillin with or without clavulanate, as well as symptomatic treatment.</p><p>Foreign body (Choice C) can present with earache, otorrhea, conductive hearing loss, or a sense of fullness in the ear, or can be asymptomatic. Children who present with ear foreign bodies should have the contralateral ear and nose examined to exclude additional foreign bodies. Extraction should be performed under direct visualization with appropriate grasping tools to minimize trauma to the ear and maximize the chance of success on the first attempt.</p><p>Labyrinthitis (Choice D), or inflammation of the inner ear, is usually viral and can present with vertigo, nystagmus, tinnitus, hearing loss, balance issues, nausea, fever, and otalgia. It should not be confused with vestibular neuritis (inflammation of the vestibular nerve alone). It may present similarly with vertigo and balance issues. However, in vestibular neuritis, hearing is not affected as the remainder of the inner ear is not involved.</p><p>Otitis externa (Choice E) presents with otalgia, otorrhea, pruritus, and hearing loss; physical examination discloses pain with ear manipulation, a normal tympanic membrane, and edema, debris, and erythema in the external auditory canal. Risk factors include foreign material in the ear and loss of cerumen through swimming. Staphylococcus aureus is a causative agent. Treatment includes removal of debris, topical antibiotics, and often a topical glucocorticoid.</p><p>Otitis media with effusion (Choice F), also termed serous otitis media, is suggested by the presence of a middle ear effusion in the absence of inflammatory signs of acute otitis media (eg, fever, tympanic membrane erythema, or retrotympanic pus). Serous fluid can accumulate within the middle ear because of eustachian tube dysfunction or obstruction, often in the setting of nasal mucosal inflammation from an upper respiratory tract infection. Treatment is supportive, as the condition is usually self-limited.</p>",
"2": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cystic fibrosis is associated with recurrent pulmonary infections caused by the impaired clearance of mucous, microbes, and debris from the airways. Recurrent infection may lead to chronic inflammatory changes in the lung parenchyma and chronic cyanosis. It is typically diagnosed using the sweat chloride test.</p><p>Cystic fibrosis is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by a defect in the CFTR gene, leading to a deficiency in a chloride channel that secretes chloride in the lungs and gastrointestinal tract, and reabsorbs chloride in sweat glands. This abnormal chloride transport causes decreased chloride and water secretion and increased water reabsorption, leading to abnormally viscous mucous. The thick mucus in the gastrointestinal tract contributes to pancreatic insufficiency, * steatorrhea, and malabsorption, leading to a deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K. In the lungs, the disease manifests as recurrent pneumonia, especially with Staphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Repeat antibiotic exposure increases the risk for colonization with resistant organisms. Obstruction and destruction of the airways leads to hypoxemia, air trapping, and bronchiectasis. Physical examination may show hypertrophic osteoarthropathy (clubbing), cyanosis, rales and rhonchi, and hyperresonance to percussion of the chest. Imaging findings include hyperinflation, bronchiectasis with peribronchial cuffing, and diffuse nonspecific inflammatory changes. Diagnosis is made using the sweat chloride test.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Measurement of serum IgE concentration (Choice A) is useful in the diagnosis of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA), a hypersensitivity reaction caused by Aspergillus fumigatus. Cystic fibrosis and ABPA share the feature of bronchiectasis, and having cystic fibrosis is a risk factor for developing ABPA. This patient's clinical presentation is more consistent with a diagnosis of cystic fibrosis.</p><p>PPD skin test (Choice B) is useful for diagnosing tuberculosis. Primary infection presents with subacute fevers, weight loss, night sweats, cough, and malaise. Suspicion would be higher in endemic areas or for patients who have spent significant time in high-risk areas.</p><p>Methacholine bronchial challenge test (Choice D) can be used to diagnose asthma, which is characterized by reversible obstruction to airflow caused by spasm of smooth muscle in the bronchi and small airways. Symptoms include dyspnea, wheezing, and/or chronic nonproductive cough. Recurrent pneumonia would be unusual.</p><p>Nasal mucosal biopsy (Choice E) is the gold standard for diagnosis of primary ciliary dyskinesia. Clinical manifestations vary, though most patients will experience recurrent upper and lower respiratory infections. Other manifestations include chronic rhinosinusitis, otitis media, and infertility. Half of patients with primary ciliary dyskinesia will have situs inversus. This patient's symptoms are more consistent with cystic fibrosis, making the sweat chloride test a more appropriate next step in diagnosis.</p>",
"3": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome commonly occurs in premature infants and presents with tachypnea, hypoxia, accessory muscle use, and cyanosis, and may lead to death. It is caused by an inadequate production of pulmonary surfactant. Lack of pulmonary surfactant leads to increased alveolar surface tension and alveolar collapse (atelectasis). Deposition of proteinaceous debris and creation of hyaline membranes within alveoli is found on biopsy.</p><p>Respiratory distress syndrome of the newborn, also called hyaline membrane disease and neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (NRDS), commonly occurs in premature infants, and is caused by an inadequate production of pulmonary surfactant. Surfactant produced by type II pneumocytes functions to decrease surface tension of the alveolar membrane. Without enough surfactant, high surface tension leads to alveolar collapse. NRDS presents with tachypnea, hypoxia, accessory muscle use, and cyanosis, and may lead to death. Chest x-ray commonly shows diffuse, bilateral airspace and interstitial opacities. Biopsy is not required for diagnosis, but if performed at autopsy, it will show atelectasis with deposition of proteinaceous alveolar debris and hyaline material. A fetus' risk for NRDS may be estimated by amniocentesis and calculation of the ratio of lecithin to sphingomyelin in the amniotic fluid. Surfactant is rich in phospholipids, such as lecithin and sphingomyelin, and an increase in the lecithin to sphingomyelin ratio correlates with increasing fetal lung development. Surfactant reaches normal concentrations of production in the third trimester, and infants who are born prior to 34 weeks' gestation are at an increased risk for NRDS. The treatment of NRDS is supportive. NRDS may also be both prevented and treated with glucocorticoids, which stimulate surfactant production.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, E, and F.</b></p><p>Bacterial pneumonia (Choice A) in the newborn can be caused by infected amniotic fluid, maternal infection, or aspiration of meconium. It commonly presents with fever or hypothermia, respiratory distress, tachycardia, and a chest x-ray with an area of consolidation. This patient is afebrile, and her chest x-ray shows diffuse opacities, rather than a focal infiltrate.</p><p>Congenital emphysema (Choice B) is a rare pulmonary disorder caused by disrupted embryonic bronchopulmonary development and is characterized by air trapping and lung hyperinflation, which can be visualized on chest imaging. The severity of disease varies widely and may be asymptomatic until adulthood or present in the newborn with respiratory distress.</p><p>Cyanotic congenital heart disease (Choice C) presents with early cyanosis (\"blue babies\"), tachypnea, feeding difficulties, and failure to thrive. The most common causes are truncus arteriosus, Tetralogy of Fallot, tricuspid atresia, transposition of the great vessels, and total anomalous pulmonary venous return.</p><p>Meconium aspiration (Choice E) occurs when the fetus inhales meconium into the lungs and is a known potential complication of labor and delivery. It presents with signs of respiratory distress (eg, cough, tachypnea, hypoxia, use of accessory muscles of respiration) and severity is a function of the volume of meconium aspirated. It is generally self-resolving when occurring in small amounts. Large aspirations can lead to pneumonia or aspiration pneumonitis.</p><p>Transient tachypnea of the newborn (Choice F) presents with tachypnea, respiratory distress, cyanosis, and hypoxia caused by a delay in the resorption of amniotic fluid in the alveoli. Risk factors include preterm delivery, maternal diabetes mellitus or asthma, and delivery via Cesarean section. Chest x-ray will show a flattened diaphragm and pulmonary vascular congestion with fluid in the interlobular fissures.</p>",
"4": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Supraventricular tachycardia, or paroxysmal episodes of rapid, narrow-complex tachycardia, presents in infants with pallor, irritability, poor feeding, and cyanosis. Diagnosis is made with electrocardiogram. Stable supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) is treated with adenosine, arteriovenous nodal blockade, or vagal maneuvers, whereas unstable SVT, characterized by hypotension and end-organ dysfunction, requires immediate cardioversion.</p><p>Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) commonly occurs in children and infants and is characterized by paroxysmal episodes of rapid, narrow-complex tachycardia. Infants often present with pallor, irritability, poor feeding, and cyanosis. SVT is often idiopathic and paroxysmal but may occur secondary to accessory pathways in some children with Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome. Diagnosis is made by electrocardiogram, although some children may require cardiac monitoring to capture a paroxysmal event. Arteriovenous (AV) nodal blockade with adenosine is the first-line treatment for narrow-complex SVT in a hemodynamically stable patient. In the AV node, binding of the adenosine receptor leads to reduced transmission of supraventricular action potentials. If adenosine fails, additional AV nodal blockers or synchronized cardioversion may be attempted. Synchronized cardioversion is also the first-line treatment in unstable SVT, as seen in this patient and suggested by the presence of hypotension and decreased end-organ perfusion (decreased urine output, lethargy, mottled skin). Children who are old enough may be trained to use vagal maneuvers to naturally induce AV nodal blockade. A similar vagal maneuver may be attempted by placing an ice bag over the eyes and face for approximately 30 seconds.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Intramuscular injection of ceftriaxone (Choice A) may be used to treat an underlying infection that could have triggered this patient's SVT, such as pneumonia. However, this patient has no signs of infection and is hemodynamically unstable. Therefore, treatment with synchronized cardioversion is the best choice.</p><p>Administration of a bolus of 0.9% saline (Choice B) may be warranted given this patient's recent decreased oral intake and number of wet diapers. However, the decrease in urine output is more likely caused by decreased organ perfusion in the setting of SVT and conversion to normal sinus rhythm may improve it without the additional administration of fluid. This patient is hemodynamically unstable and requires immediate cardioversion.</p><p>Chest compressions (Choice C) are not warranted in this patient. In infants, chest compressions are started for cardiac arrest. This patient has a heart rate and blood pressure, although the latter is inadequate for end-organ perfusion caused by SVT. Performing chest compressions in a patient with organized cardiac activity can be harmful. Cardioversion is the more appropriate first step.</p><p>Endotracheal intubation (Choice E) is not necessary currently as this patient appears to be adequately protecting his airway, and his lungs are clear to auscultation. Pulse oximetry is difficult to obtain solely given his rapid heartbeat, and cardioversion would improve this. Additionally, giving this patient sedation to allow optimal intubating conditions may worsen his hypotension and cause cardiac arrest.</p>",
"5": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Von Willebrand disease results from impairments in the amount or function of the von Willebrand protein multimer, a plasma protein that binds platelets to subendothelial collagen. The deficiency results in impaired hemostasis, generally involving mucosal and epithelial locations, caused by deficiency of platelet adhesion. The platelet count is generally normal, and the partial thromboplastin time is increased because of the protein's role in shuttling factor VIII in plasma. Treatment includes supportive care plus supplemental desmopressin.</p><p>The most likely diagnosis in this patient with menorrhagia leading to anemia and an isolated increase in partial thromboplastin time (PTT) is von Willebrand disease (VWD), a common coagulopathy affecting platelet qualitative function. VWD results from impairments in the amount or function of the von Willebrand protein multimer, a plasma protein that binds platelets to subendothelial collagen. The deficiency results in impaired hemostasis, generally involving mucosal and epithelial locations, due to deficiency of platelet adhesion. VWD can be genetic (autosomal dominant) or acquired. Since the von Willebrand multimer also acts as a serum transport protein for coagulation factor VIII, factor VIII concentrations may be decreased, leading to the increase in PTT without affecting the prothrombin time (PT). Patients often present with menorrhagia, recurrent nosebleeds, easy bruising, petechiae, gastrointestinal, and gingival bleeding. Unless blood loss is substantial, laboratory studies will otherwise be normal. Mild VWD is generally asymptomatic, although moderate or severe deficiency can result in transfusion-dependent anemia, unabating hemorrhage, and shock. Treatment, in addition to supportive care with bleeding control and transfusions, may involve the administration of desmopressin, which promotes the release of additional vWF stored in endothelial cells.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Acute lymphocytic leukemia (Choice A) is most common in pediatric patients and presents with weight loss, fevers, and malaise. Anemia and thrombocytopenia can also occur, leading to easy bruising and petechiae. This patient has a clear source of bleeding leading to her anemia with a normal concentration of platelets and no systemic symptoms, making von Willebrand disease a more likely diagnosis.</p><p>Anovulatory menstrual cycles (Choice B) can cause menorrhagia caused by prolonged proliferation of the endometrial lining. However, anovulatory menstrual cycles are generally associated with irregularly timed intervals between periods. This patient has had a normal cycle length and recurrence, making anovulatory cycles an unlikely cause of her menorrhagia. They would also not describe the increase in this patient's PTT.</p><p>Factor XI deficiency (Choice C) is an uncommon coagulopathy caused by genetic or acquired quantitative or qualitative compromise of factor XI. It may result in bleeding, such as that seen in this patient. However, bleeding associated with factor deficiency more commonly occurs early in life and in visceral as opposed to mucosal locations.</p><p>Factor XIII deficiency (Choice D) is a rare coagulopathy that leads to spontaneous bleeding, including hemarthrosis and intracranial hemorrhages. While it can cause menorrhagia, it more commonly presents early in life. Factor XIII deficiency would also cause a prolonged PT and PTT.</p><p>Hyperthyroidism (Choice E) can lead to menorrhagia via anovulatory cycles, which often cause irregular and heavy menstrual bleeding. However, other symptoms of hyperthyroidism, such as hot flashes, weight loss, tachycardia, and tremors are not present in this patient.</p>",
"6": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Alcohol is a known teratogen that causes fetal alcohol syndrome when used during pregnancy. Fetal alcohol syndrome presents with intellectual developmental disorder along with characteristic physical malformations, such as facial dysmorphia (eg, small palpebral fissures, thin vermillion border, smooth philtrum), limb and muscular deformities, and congenital heart disease.</p><p>Alcohol is a proven teratogen, as alcohol use during pregnancy leads to irreversible fetal neurological effects and physical malformations in a cluster of symptoms called fetal alcohol syndrome. Due to the disruption of cell migration during development, fetal alcohol syndrome presents with physical abnormalities, such as microcephaly, facial abnormalities (eg, small palpebral fissures, maxillary hypoplasia, thin vermilion border, cleft lip/palate, smooth philtrum), limb deformities, and congenital heart disease. Fetal alcohol syndrome is the leading cause of intellectual disability in the United States. Muscular abnormalities (eg, gross motor delays, hypotonia, tremulousness) are also common. Patients with fetal alcohol syndrome may demonstrate abnormal bone development (eg, cervical spine fusion) and delayed bone growth.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Congenital toxoplasmosis (Choice A) typically presents with features of chorioretinitis, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, convulsions, cerebral calcifications, and hydrocephalus.</p><p>Down syndrome (Choice B) is a chromosomal trisomy that presents with hypotonia, downturned palpebral fissures, prominent epicanthal folds, flat facies, single palmar creases, and intellectual developmental disorder. It increases the risk for congenital cardiac and gastrointestinal malformations. This patient has dysmorphic features consistent with fetal alcohol syndrome (eg, small palpebral fissures rather than downturned palpebral fissures).</p><p>Fragile X syndrome (Choice D) is an X-linked dominant cause of inherited intellectual disability that presents with intellectual developmental disorder, an elongated face with a prominent jaw, a high-arched palate, large ears, hyperextensible joints, and postpubertal macroorchidism. This patient's jaw size is instead decreased, and he does not demonstrate hyperextensible joints.</p><p>Seminiferous tubule dysgenesis (Klinefelter syndrome) (Choice E) features an XXY karyotype, which causes dysgenesis of the seminiferous tubules and testicular atrophy, leading to decreased spermatogenesis. Dysmorphic body features including a eunuchoid body shape, long extremities, and gynecomastia would be observed. This patient does not demonstrate dysmorphic features consistent with seminiferous tubule dysgenesis. Trisomy 13 (Choice F), or Patau syndrome, presents with intellectual developmental disorder and characteristic physical features including cleft lip and palate, holoprosencephaly, microphthalmia, cutis aplasia, feet with a prominent calcaneus and convexly rounded soles, and polydactyly. This patient does not demonstrate gross neurologic abnormalities consistent with holoprosencephaly or cutis aplasia.</p>",
"7": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Rickets is most commonly caused by childhood vitamin D deficiency, which impairs calcium and phosphate homeostasis and bone mineralization. This presents as frontal bossing, bone pain and deformities, such as bowing of the legs, widening of zones of mineralization on radiography due to unmineralized osteoid, hypoplastic teeth, bulging of costochondral junctions, and growth delay. Breastfed infants are at risk for vitamin D deficiency and are recommended to receive oral vitamin D supplementation.</p><p>Alkaline phosphatase is an enzyme present in bile ductules and bone. It plays a role in bone mineralization by osteoblasts, though the exact mechanism is unknown. Vitamin D plays a role in serum calcium and phosphate homeostasis by promoting intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate, increasing bone mineralization at low concentrations, and increasing bone resorption at higher concentrations. Parathyroid hormone (PTH) also has a role in calcium and phosphate regulation by stimulating osteoclastic bone reabsorption and calcium reabsorption and phosphate excretion in the kidney. Vitamin D deficiency can be caused by malabsorption in the intestinal tract, malnutrition or insufficient dietary intake, and decreased sun exposure. Decreased concentrations of vitamin D result in decreased intestinal calcium absorption and hypocalcemia, sensed by the parathyroid gland via calcium-sensing receptors, leading to an increase in secretion of PTH to normalize serum calcium concentrations. Increased PTH results in increased osteoclast activity, intestinal calcium absorption, and kidney tubular absorption of calcium and excretion of phosphorus. Increased osteoclast activity leads to increased bone turnover, which releases calcium and alkaline phosphatase into the bloodstream. This leads to rickets in children, characterized by frontal bossing, bone pain and deformities such as bowing of the legs, widening of zones of mineralization on radiography caused by unmineralized osteoid, hypoplastic teeth, bulging of costochondral junctions, and growth delay. In infants, such as this patient, bone pain may manifest as refusal or inability to crawl or walk. Because of the risk for vitamin D deficiency, breastfed infants are recommended to receive oral vitamin D supplementation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>ALT activity (Choice A) will be increased along with AST activity in conditions that damage the liver, such as hepatitis and medication-induced hepatotoxicity. Increased alkaline phosphatase can be indicative of liver or biliary disease. Measurements of other liver enzymes, such as AST and ALT, as well as gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase, can distinguish the source of increased alkaline phosphatase.</p><p>Calcium concentration (Choice C) is likely normal or decreased because this patient's intestinal calcium absorption is low. Hypercalcemia and bone turnover are features of primary hyperparathyroidism. Manifestations of hypercalcemia include nephrolithiasis, abdominal pain, constipation, pancreatitis, polyuria, and altered mental status.</p><p>Magnesium concentration (Choice D) would not be affected in rickets as this mineral does not play a significant role in bone or vitamin D metabolism. Hypermagnesemia can be seen in conditions of high cell turnover, such as tumor lysis syndrome. Hypermagnesemia can inhibit PTH secretion. Over time, this can lead to hypocalcemia and bone disease.</p><p>Phosphorus concentration (Choice E) will be normal or low in this patient due to decreased vitamin D and increased PTH. Hyperphosphatemia is associated with hypoparathyroidism. Low PTH leads to impaired kidney phosphorus excretion. Serum phosphorus is also increased in secondary hyperparathyroidism due to chronic kidney disease, as impaired phosphorus excretion results in binding of serum calcium by phosphate and increased PTH, which leads to osteodystrophy.</p>",
"8": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Primary enuresis refers to episodic urinary incontinence occurring in children older than 5 years who have never remained dry through the night. The pathogenesis of primary enuresis most commonly involves a delay in maturation.</p><p>Primary enuresis refers to episodic urinary incontinence occurring in children older than age 5 years who have never remained dry through the night. The pathogenesis of primary enuresis most commonly involves a delay in maturation, though may alternatively be related to a small functional bladder capacity or genetic factors. The episodes commonly lead to adjustment problems and low self-esteem. Along with a voiding diary, patients with nocturnal enuresis should undergo a thorough history, physical examination, and urinalysis to rule out medical causes of enuresis (eg, bladder dysfunction, urinary tract infection). Many patients with primary enuresis spontaneously improve, though motivational therapy (eg, rewarding the patient for urinating prior to bed and/or having incontinence-free nights) may hasten improvement. In patients with persistent enuresis or enuresis associated with emotional problems, management with enuresis alarms or desmopressin is indicated.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Adjustment disorder (Choice A) refers to the onset of disproportionate distress within 3 months of an identifiable stressor. Adjustment disorders are typically associated with anxiety, depression, or behavioral symptoms without meeting the criteria of anxiety or depressive disorders. Though the patient is having difficulty making friends, he does not show depressive or anxiety symptoms.</p><p>Detrusor muscle overactivity (Choice B) results in urge incontinence. This patient denies urinary urgency.</p><p>Primary sleep disorder (Choice D) is characterized by insomnia or dysregulated sleep, not heavy sleeping. Primary enuresis is not characterized as a sleep disorder.</p><p>Urinary tract infections (Choice E) present with dysuria, frequency, urgency, and suprapubic discomfort. They occur more frequently in women than men because of female anatomical factors such as a shorter urethra and favorable regional environment for bacterial growth. This patient denies dysuria and urgency. In addition, this patient's urinalysis did not demonstrate leukocyte esterase or nitrites, which are commonly seen in a urinary tract infection.</p>",
"9": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Gonadal dysgenesis 45,X (Turner syndrome) is a cause of primary amenorrhea, which is secondary to ovarian dysgenesis in the setting of a 45,XO genotype. Other signs of gonadal dysgenesis 45,X include a short stature, cystic hygroma or webbed neck, swelling in the hands/feet, coarctation of the aorta, bicuspid aortic valve, horseshoe kidney, infertility, and lack of secondary sexual characteristics. Diagnosis is made with a karyotype analysis.</p><p>Gonadal dysgenesis 45,X (Turner syndrome) is a cause of primary amenorrhea, which is secondary to ovarian dysgenesis in the setting of a 45,XO genotype. Other signs of gonadal dysgenesis 45,X include a short stature, cystic hygroma or webbed neck, swelling in the hands/feet, coarctation of the aorta, bicuspid aortic valve, horseshoe kidney, and infertility. Additionally, because of decreased estrogen concentrations, patients fail to develop secondary sexual characteristics, such as breasts, and demonstrate an underdeveloped uterus. Vital signs will be normal, and diagnosis is made through a karyotype analysis. Other laboratory studies that may aid in diagnosis are increased concentrations of luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) caused by the lack of negative feedback from estrogen. Gonadal dysgenesis 45,X management in adolescents commonly involves monitoring for cardiac complications, growth hormone replacement, and estrogen replacement to promote increased stature and development of secondary sexual characteristics.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Measurement of serum progesterone concentration (Choice B) is not useful in the diagnosis of gonadal dysgenesis 45,X. While other laboratory studies may be warranted in the evaluation of primary amenorrhea, such as human chorionic gonadotropin, FSH, luteinizing hormone, thyroid-stimulating hormone, and prolactin, progesterone is not typically measured. Progesterone deficiency or excess would also not explain this patient's decreased development of secondary sexual characteristics.</p><p>Vaginal cytology (Choice C) is not commonly performed. While vaginal cytology can help evaluate changes in estrogen production, it is not useful in the evaluation of primary amenorrhea.</p><p>Hysterosalpingography (Choice D) can be used to evaluate the physical structure of the vagina, uterus, and fallopian tubes. Anatomical defects, such as an absent uterus or transverse vaginal septum, may cause amenorrhea. A karyotype analysis is less invasive, and if positive, would also explain this patient's lack of secondary sexual characteristics.</p><p>CT scan of the head (Choice E) would be used to evaluate intracranial abnormalities that could cause amenorrhea, such as a prolactinoma. Prolactinomas cause hyperprolactinemia, which inhibits the release of hormones such as FSH and LH, thereby leading to amenorrhea. Prolactinomas often also present with galactorrhea, headaches, and visual disturbances, which this patient does not have.</p>",
"10": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Respiratory syncytial virus is one of the most common causes of bronchiolitis and pneumonia in children younger than two years of age.</p><p>Bronchiolitis is a common cause of lower respiratory tract infection in children under age 2 years. It is typically caused by a viral infection, most commonly from respiratory syncytial virus. It presents with a viral prodrome of fever, nasal congestion, and rhinorrhea followed by cough, dyspnea, and lethargy as inflammation progresses to the lower airway. Auscultation may disclose wheezes and crackles. Severe disease is marked by the presence of increased respiratory effort (tachypnea, accessory muscle use, intercostal retractions, nasal flaring, grunting), hypoxemia, and apnea. Patients presenting with severe disease should be hospitalized and immediately started on respiratory support with the administration of oxygen (generally via high-flow nasal cannula or advanced noninvasive positive pressure ventilation), suctioning, and consideration of advanced airway management if respiratory failure is imminent.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, E, G, and H.</b></p><p>Pertussis occurs caused by an infection from Bordetella pertussis (Choice A) and may lead to severe paroxysmal coughing, which may be accompanied by cyanosis and followed by syncope, emesis, or apnea. Pertussis can be prevented with immunization and is treated with antibiotics, typically macrolides.</p><p>Chlamydia trachomatis (Choice B) is a common cause of sexually transmitted infections as well as neonatal conjunctivitis. Chlamydial conjunctivitis presents within 5 to 14 days of birth and is characterized by watery discharge.</p><p>Influenza virus (Choice C) is a common cause of viral pneumonia during the winter months. Bronchiolitis is more commonly caused by respiratory syncytial virus.</p><p>Listeria monocytogenes (Choice D) is a common cause of neonatal meningitis or pneumonia. Meningitis typically presents with lethargy, poor feeding, bulging fontanelle, altered mental status, and seizures. Pneumonia from Listeria generally occurs before 1 to 2 months of age.</p><p>Mycoplasma pneumoniae (Choice E) can cause pneumonia in children, with fever, cough, and crackles on auscultation of the lungs. In general, bronchiolitis is triggered by viral pathogens more frequently.</p><p>Staphylococcus aureus (Choice G) is more commonly associated with skin and soft tissue infections, septic arthritis, and enteritis. It can cause pneumonia, though does so more frequently in post-viral superinfections or in the immunocompromised. It also colonizes the lung in patients with cystic fibrosis. It would be an unlikely pathogen to infect an otherwise healthy child.</p><p>Streptococcus pneumoniae (Choice H) is a common bacterial cause of pneumonia, but patients tend to present with focal lung findings indicating a lobar pneumonia as well as a lobar consolidation on chest x-ray. Diffuse wheezing on examination and peribronchial thickening on chest x-ray are more consistent with a diagnosis of bronchiolitis.</p>",
"11": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Increased platelet destruction in immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) is associated with the development of antibodies that bind platelets, leading to opsonization and phagocytosis by macrophages in the spleen. ITP is diagnosed in patients with a low platelet count without alternative explanatory causes, and presents with petechiae and purpura, often in patients who appear otherwise well. Acute ITP may follow an infection in children, generally is self-limited, and requires clinical monitoring.</p><p>Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) is caused by antiplatelet antibodies that bind platelets, leading to opsonization and destruction. Platelet destruction occurs when macrophages within the spleen phagocytose the platelet-antibody complex, resulting in thrombocytopenia. ITP is diagnosed in the setting of a low platelet count and absence of other explanatory causes for thrombocytopenia. Bone marrow biopsy will classically show an increased number of megakaryocytes indicating adequate platelet production but increased peripheral destruction of platelets. It classically presents with petechiae, purpura, and prolonged bleeding time on laboratory analysis, although bleeding time is rarely measured in routine clinical practice. Severe ITP may lead to uncontrolled hemorrhage. Acute ITP is often seen in pediatric patients, may follow an infection, generally is self-limited, and is managed conservatively in the absence of life-threatening bleeds.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Abnormal platelet function (Choice A) may be seen in genetic conditions such as Glanzmann thrombasthenia, in which patients have a defect in the GpIIb/IIIa protein, preventing both platelet aggregation and formation of a platelet plug.</p><p>Child abuse (Choice B) is associated with the development of both unexplained ecchymosis and bone fractures at different stages of healing, as well as cerebral hematomas. Patients suspected of child abuse should undergo a complete blood count to rule out a bleeding disorder, skeletal survey, and a noncontrast CT scan of the head.</p><p>Decreased platelet production (Choice C) may be the result of conditions including aplastic anemia. This condition typically presents with anemia, thrombocytopenia, and leukopenia. It may be associated with parvovirus B19 infection in children, especially those with underlying sickle cell anemia.</p><p>Disseminated intravascular coagulation (Choice D) is a systemic disorder characterized by the widespread activation of the clotting cascade resulting in microthrombi formation, thrombocytopenia, and bleeding complications due to consumption of platelets and clotting factors. It can be provoked by trauma, sepsis, malignancy, pancreatitis, exposure to toxins, and multiple other causes. Laboratory studies show thrombocytopenia, prolonged prothrombin time and activated partial thromboplastin time, decreased fibrinogen concentrations, decreased platelet count, and increased D-dimer concentrations (fibrin split products).</p>",
"12": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: An adrenal adenoma arising from the zona reticularis of the adrenal medulla produces high levels of dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate leading to hirsutism, virilization, and increased 17-ketosteroid concentrations. These tumors should be suspected in female patients with hirsutism and virilization that have a normal pelvic ultrasound, normal testosterone concentrations, and increased dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate concentrations.</p><p>The adrenal cortex is composed of three zones including the zona glomerulosa producing aldosterone, zona fasciculata producing cortisol, and zona reticularis producing dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate. In the zona reticularis, 17-hydroxypregnenolone is converted to dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate by 17α-hydroxylase. Patients who present with virilization of the female genitalia, hirsutism, and elevation of dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate and 17-ketosteroid excretion should be evaluated for the development of an adrenal adenoma. These patients will have normal testosterone concentrations as well as normal pelvic ultrasound.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Androgen insensitivity syndrome (Choice B) results from defect in the androgen receptor. Genotypically male patients with this condition exhibit a normal-appearing female phenotype with female external genitalia and rudimentary vagina but internally develop normal functioning testes. This patient does not display the physical characteristics of androgen insensitivity syndrome.</p><p>An ovarian adenoma (Choice C), specifically a Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor, produces androgens including testosterone, which may present with hirsutism and virilization. Patients with Sertoli-Leydig cell tumors present with increased testosterone, making the diagnosis of ovarian adenoma unlikely.</p><p>Pituitary adenoma (Choice D), including prolactinoma, can present with bilateral peripheral vision loss, headache, amenorrhea, and galactorrhea in female patients. A pituitary adenoma leading to virilization would be less common as most functional adenomas produce prolactin or growth hormone.</p><p>Polycystic ovarian syndrome (Choice E) presents with hirsutism, increased body weight, and anovulation, leading to irregular menstrual bleeding and infertility. Ultrasound of the pelvis commonly shows enlarged ovaries containing numerous follicular cysts. Polycystic ovarian syndrome presents with an increased luteinizing hormone to follicle-stimulating hormone ratio, as well as increased testosterone.</p>",
"13": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Posterior urethral valves are formed from an abnormal remnant of the mesonephric (wolffian) duct and can result in urinary tract obstruction. Signs and symptoms can include bladder distention, hydronephrosis, oligohydramnios, and a fetus that is small for gestational age.</p><p>Congenital urinary tract abnormalities include but are not limited to unilateral renal agenesis, fused kidneys, ureteral stricture and stenosis, duplex collecting system, posterior urethral valves, bladder agenesis, bladder exstrophy, hypo- or epispadias, and urethral strictures. Posterior urethral valves are formed from an abnormal remnant of the mesonephric (wolffian) duct and can result in urinary tract obstruction. This is often diagnosed on prenatal ultrasonography showing bladder distention, hydronephrosis, oligohydramnios, and a fetus that is small for gestational age. Some of these features were notable in this patient's case. In this patient, an abnormal voiding pattern plus postnatal ultrasonography suggest posterior urethral valves as a cause of urinary obstruction. Complications include rupture of a calyx around the kidney, bladder rupture, and kidney failure. Definitive diagnosis of posterior urethral valves is made with voiding cystourethrogram showing dilation, elongation, and tapering of the urethra. Treatment is with endoscopic valve ablation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>A duplicated renal collecting system (Choice A) would be revealed on ultrasonography as two ureters exiting one kidney. Sometimes they are asymptomatic, but also may be complicated by hydronephrosis or urinary tract infections.</p><p>Neurogenic bladder (Choice B) leads to overflow incontinence, which is characterized by chronic urinary retention and a chronically distended bladder. When intravesical pressure exceeds outlet resistance, incontinence results as urine flows. Overflow incontinence may be caused by physical barriers, such as tumors or urethral stricture, neurologic lesions, or as a side effect of some medications.</p><p>Ureteropelvic junction obstruction (Choice D) describes a blockage between the ureter and the renal pelvis, which leads to hydronephrosis but not hydroureter.</p><p>Vesicoureteral reflux (Choice E) can also lead to bilateral hydronephrosis. However, ultrasonography showing a thickened bladder wall is more associated with posterior urethral valves. Patients with vesicoureteral reflux are at an increased risk for recurrent urinary tract infections, including pyelonephritis, which can lead to complications such as kidney scarring, hypertension, and chronic kidney disease.</p>",
"14": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute gastroenteritis is a common, typically self-limiting illness in children characterized by nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and abdominal pain. Compared to adults, pediatric patients are at increased risk for volume depletion. Oral rehydration therapy is preferred for mild and moderate cases of hypovolemia, while isotonic intravenous fluids should be used for severe hypovolemia.</p><p>Acute-onset fever, emesis, and diarrhea is consistent with a likely diagnosis of gastroenteritis. Though this is typically a self-limiting illness, children with gastroenteritis are at increased risk for volume depletion in comparison to adults, and this patient should be treated initially with oral hydration solution. Clinical signs of volume depletion include tachycardia, tachypnea, hypotension, dry mucous membranes, poor skin turgor, and prolonged capillary refill. Children may present with irritability or lethargy. Laboratory evaluation may show perturbations in serum sodium, bicarbonate, and potassium concentrations. Volume depletion secondary to diarrhea is usually accompanied by a significant stool loss of bicarbonate, which in combination with the hypoperfusion of end organs, may result in metabolic acidosis. Urinalysis shows aciduria and a high urinary specific gravity. Treatment includes the repletion of fluids and electrolytes.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Oral bismuth subsalicylate (Choice A) is a nonantibiotic treatment option for adults with acute gastroenteritis but should be avoided in infants and pregnant women because of the risk for salicylate toxicity.</p><p>Oral loperamide (Choice C) and other antidiarrheal agents are generally not recommended for treatment of acute gastroenteritis in children. The condition is usually self-limiting, and there is little known benefit of using these agents with significant risk for side effects.</p><p>Rectal prochlorperazine (Choice D) is an antipsychotic and effective antiemetic typically reserved for severe, refractory nausea and vomiting, chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting, and intractable migraine in children. Intravenous 5% dextrose in water (Choice E), or any isotonic intravenous fluid, is indicated for severe volume depletion in children, though when feasible and tolerated, oral rehydration therapy is preferred. Severe volume depletion is characterized by delayed capillary refill time (greater than 3 seconds), cold and mottled extremities, lethargy, and hypovolemic shock.</p>",
"15": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Rotavirus is a double-stranded RNA virus that causes infectious diarrhea in children. The diarrhea caused by rotavirus is watery, profuse, and often foul-smelling, and is typically not bloody. Rotavirus vaccine is available in a variety of live-attenuated formulations and is recommended for all children. The vaccine is given in a two or three dose series between age 2 and 6 months.</p><p>Rotavirus is a double-stranded RNA virus that causes infectious diarrhea in children. Rotavirus infection is the most common cause of diarrheal illness in children in the world and is commonly acquired during the first several years of life. The virus is transmitted fecal-orally and is highly contagious. Viral shedding in the stool can occur prior to the development of symptoms and potentially allow asymptomatic patients to become vectors for disease. The diarrhea caused by rotavirus is watery, profuse, and often foul-smelling, and is typically not bloody. While most cases are self-resolving, severe infections may produce dehydration, which may be life-threatening, particularly in low-resource settings and developing countries. Rotavirus vaccine is available in a variety of live-attenuated formulations and is recommended for all children. The vaccine is given in a two or three dose series between age 2 and 6 months.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Chickenpox (Choice A) is caused by infection with varicella virus. Vaccine against varicella is administered in a two-dose series at age 12 months and between age 4 to 6 years.</p><p>Influenza (Choice B) vaccine is recommended for prevention of influenza beginning at age greater than 6 months.</p><p>Measles-mumps-rubella vaccine (Choice C) prevents measles, mumps, and rubella infection, which can cause different viral syndromes. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends vaccination with measles-mumps-rubella in a two-dose series beginning at age 1 year.</p><p>Shingles (Choice E) occurs because of infection with varicella virus and presents in elderly patients as a painful, unilateral, dermatomal erythematous patch with overlying clear to yellow, grouped vesicles. The CDC recommends shingles vaccination beginning at age 60 years.</p>",
"16": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Excessive fluid intake may lead to dilutional hyponatremia. The abrupt decrease in serum osmolality can lead to cerebral edema and subsequent altered mental status. Treatment for severe hyponatremia with altered mental status includes administration of hypertonic saline.</p><p>This patient with altered mental status and edema in the setting of recent electrolyte depletion from gastroenteritis followed by large volume water intake likely has hyponatremia. Excessive fluid intake can lead to dilutional hyponatremia, likely worsened by the now resolved volume depletion from the patient's previous illness. An abrupt decrease in serum osmolality leads to transcellular shifting of fluid by osmosis with neuronal swelling in the central nervous system, which can lead to findings ranging from mild confusion and disorientation to brain herniation. Treatment for hypervolemic hyponatremia depends on the severity of symptoms and specific cause (eg, heart failure vs kidney or liver failure). If significant altered mental status is present, treatment involves administration of hypertonic saline to correct osmolar shifts and address severe neurologic symptoms and prevent seizures caused by hyponatremia. Management for mild hypervolemic hyponatremia involves water restriction and diuretics.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Dehydration (Choice A) can lead to headache, weakness, and nausea. If severe, dehydration can lead to hypotension, and subsequent cerebral hypoperfusion may lead to disorientation. This patient's history of large volume water intake along with altered mentation in the setting of normal blood pressure make dehydration unlikely.</p><p>Heat exhaustion (Choice B) is characterized by exercise intolerance, increased core body temperature, generalized weakness, and muscle cramps, and the absence of neurologic findings.</p><p>Heat stroke (Choice C) occurs in the setting of high environmental temperatures and high humidity. Excessive heat exposure and increased core body temperature leads to cardiovascular and neurologic dysfunction. It is characterized by hyperthermia, altered mental status or neurologic deficits, and variable physical signs and symptoms depending on severity. This patient's history of recent electrolyte depletion followed by large volume water intake make hyponatremia more likely.</p><p>Hypoglycemia (Choice D) can cause confusion, altered mental status, and weakness. The patient would be at risk for hypoglycemia caused by exercising after not eating since breakfast. However, healthy individuals are typically able to maintain a stable serum glucose concentration because of hepatic glycogenolysis during periods of exercise and fasting.</p>",
"17": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Renal tubular acidosis results in a hyperchloremic, non-anion gap metabolic acidosis from dysregulation of the kidney tubules and is associated with a compensatory respiratory alkalosis and hypokalemia.</p><p>Renal tubular acidosis (RTA) results in a hyperchloremic, non-anion gap metabolic acidosis from dysregulation of the kidney tubules. There are three types of RTA, varying in cause and pathophysiology, but all result in a non-anion gap metabolic acidosis. This patient shows a low bicarbonate concentration, with a normal anion gap, consistent with a non-anion gap acidosis. Distal RTA (type 1) is caused by a defect in kidney secretion of H+, which results in the lack of generation of HCO3−, ultimately leading to a metabolic acidosis. It is characterized by a urine pH &gt; 5.5. The most common causes of type 1 RTA include analgesic nephropathy, congenital obstructive urinary tract anomalies, drugs (eg, amphotericin), and autoimmune diseases resulting in hypergammaglobulinemia (eg, rheumatoid arthritis, Sjogren syndrome). This patient's arterial blood gas shows respiratory compensation, though incomplete. RTA is associated with kidney potassium wasting and hypokalemia.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, E, F, G, and H.</b></p><p>Addison disease (Choice A), or primary adrenal insufficiency, is commonly due to autoimmune destruction or tuberculosis. It presents with hypotension, hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, and skin hyperpigmentation secondary to a deficiency of aldosterone and cortisol.</p><p>Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (Choice B) results from a defect in adrenal steroid biosynthesis. Its most common form, 21-hydroxylase deficiency, presents with salt-wasting in infants or precocious puberty in children. It also causes the virilization of XX fetuses, increased levels of 17-hydroxyprogesterone, hypotension, and hyperkalemia. Treatment includes the exogenous administration of mineralocorticoids and glucocorticoids.</p><p>Hyperparathyroidism (Choice C) leads to bony resorption and hypercalcemia through increased production of parathyroid hormone. Primary hyperparathyroidism results from a functionally active parathyroid adenoma, hyperplasia, or carcinoma. Secondary hyperparathyroidism is commonly due to chronic hypocalcemia in the setting of end-stage kidney disease.</p><p>Juxtaglomerular cell hyperplasia (Choice D) is responsible for the production of renin, leading to increased angiotensin II, which is a potent vasoconstrictor that directly mediates systemic hypertension. Angiotensin II also leads to increased Na+ reabsorption in the kidney tubules, aldosterone release by the adrenal cortex with subsequent increased Na+ and fluid retention and hypokalemia. It would not cause a metabolic acidosis or compensatory respiratory alkalosis.</p><p>Pheochromocytoma (Choice E) presents with episodes of increased blood pressure, headache, and palpitations, commonly due to a tumor of the adrenal medulla. It does not cause hypokalemia, and the symptoms would be episodic.</p><p>Primary hyperaldosteronism (Choice F) presents with hypertension, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and an increased aldosterone-to-renin ratio due to the independent secretion of aldosterone. It is most associated with an adrenal adenoma or bilateral adrenal hyperplasia.</p><p>Pseudohypoaldosteronism (Choice G) is characterized by problems with sodium regulation leading to hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis.</p><p>Renal artery stenosis (Choice H) can lead to chronic kidney disease, which presents with increased creatinine, electrolyte and acid-base derangements, and hypertension.</p>",
"18": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pulmonary stenosis is a congenital, benign cardiac malformation that may occur in isolation or as part of a congenital cardiac syndrome. The stenosis may occur at the level of the right ventricular outflow tract, pulmonic valve, or pulmonary artery. A systolic ejection murmur with radiation to the back may be heard. Prognosis is excellent with corrective intervention.</p><p>The most likely diagnosis is pulmonary stenosis. It is often diagnosed early in life and in female patients more than male. Pulmonary stenosis is typically congenital and benign, or it may be associated with other congenital cardiac syndromes. The blockage may occur at the level of the pulmonic valve as a result of fusion of the commissures or other defects of the leaflets, subvalvular caused by narrowing of the right ventricular outflow tract, or supravalvular (also called peripheral pulmonic stenosis) caused by narrowing or impingement of the pulmonary artery. Physical examination findings depend on the severity of stenosis. Most patients will have a systolic ejection murmur with radiation to the back. Some may have an associated ejection click, split S2, or left parasternal heave. Most patients are asymptomatic, though complications can occur if the condition persists into adulthood. Surgical revision and balloon valvotomy have excellent prognosis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Aortic stenosis (Choice A) presents with a crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur, best heard at the upper right sternal border, and radiates to the carotid arteries. In a newborn, it may be caused by a congenital bicuspid aortic valve or other congenital cardiac syndromes.</p><p>Atrial septal defect (Choice B) is a common congenital malformation of the interatrial septum resulting in a left to right shunt. Examination typically shows a fixed, widely split S2 caused by increased blood flow through the pulmonic valve. Severe defects can result in pulmonary hypertension and development of Eisenmenger syndrome over time, with reversal of the left to right shunt.</p><p>Patent ductus arteriosus (Choice C) results from failure of the normal, extracardiac embryonic shunt between the pulmonary artery and aorta to appropriately close in the neonatal period. It results in an extracardiac left-to-right shunt and classically presents with a continuous machine-like murmur.</p><p>Ventricular septal defect (Choice E) are common cardiac malformations that result in a left-to-right intracardiac shunt. They may form in isolation or in conjunction with other malformations. They are characterized by a holosystolic murmur best heard in the left lower sternal border and may have an associated palpable thrill.</p>",
"19": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Intussusception occurs when a segment of bowel telescopes into an adjacent segment, resulting in colicky abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and potential bloody, mucoid stools. A barium enema is useful in the diagnosis as well as treatment for intussusception.</p><p>Intussusception occurs when a segment of bowel telescopes into an adjacent segment, resulting in colicky abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and potential bloody, mucoid stools. Classically, it presents in an infant or young child with recurrent episodes of severe abdominal pain often punctuated by asymptomatic periods. Intussusception typically occurs due to the presence of a pathologic lead point, often lymphoid hyperplasia, lymphadenopathy, Meckel diverticulum, stricture, or malignancy, which acts to draw one segment of bowel into an adjacent segment during peristalsis. If the intussusception self-resolves, there may be no symptoms. If it does not resolve, traction on mesenteric vessels can result in hypoperfusion of the segment with resultant inflammation, hemorrhage, obstruction, and pain. It may be detected on physical examination as an elongated mass, often in the right upper quadrant. Diagnosis consists of clinical history, physical examination, and is supported by imaging. Ultrasound evaluation is preferred in young children and will show telescoping interposed bowel segments in longitudinal orientation, or a targetoid appearance in transverse orientation. A contrast enema can also demonstrate intussusception with appearance of a mass prolapsing into the lumen and an inability of the contrast to reach the proximal bowel. Treatment of an ileocolic intussusception requires the administration of a contrast enema, which reduces the intussusception. Contrast enema is both diagnostic and therapeutic in this setting. Surgery may be required in cases where there is repetitive failure of the enema to reduce the intussusception or if the patient presents with symptoms concerning for bowel necrosis or perforation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>A bleeding diverticulum (Choice A) is a common cause of painless lower gastrointestinal bleeding in older adults. In children, a Meckel diverticulum can bleed, though it would not present with signs of obstruction as in this case.</p><p>Malrotation volvulus (Choice C) occurs secondary to the incomplete rotation of bowel during embryonic development. This places the newborn at risk for midgut volvulus, which can present with bilious vomiting, abdominal distension, and pain. In severe cases, presentation may also include intestinal necrosis, hematochezia, perforation, and peritonitis.</p><p>A peptic ulcer (Choice D) is uncommon in children and can be related to nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs or Helicobacter pylori infection. Patients typically present with epigastric discomfort, nausea, and vomiting, typically after eating. Peptic ulcer disease can be complicated by hemorrhage, typically resulting in melena or perforation.</p><p>Pyloric stenosis (Choice E) presents at several weeks of life and is characterized by repeated nonbilious vomiting after feeds, leading to dehydration and a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. Diagnosis is made with ultrasonography, and treatment is with surgical pyloromyotomy.</p>",
"20": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Slipped capital femoral epiphysis results in displacement of the femoral epiphysis relative to the femoral neck, and management involves surgical fixation of the femoral epiphysis.</p><p>Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE) most commonly occurs in overweight children between the ages of 10 to 15. It results in displacement of the femoral epiphysis relative to the femoral neck secondary to anterosuperior movement of the metaphysis because of a Salter-Harris type 1 fracture of the growth plate. SCFE typically presents with a painful limp, and if severe, may result in restricted range of motion of the hip joint (eg, limited abduction and internal rotation of the hip) and inability to bear weight on the affected lower extremity. Diagnosis requires evaluation of both hips in frontal and frog-leg views for comparison. If untreated, complications include avascular necrosis of the femoral head, limited range of motion and gait impairment, and premature osteoarthritis. To prevent these complications, treatment requires surgical fixation of the femoral epiphysis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Physical therapy (Choice A) would be appropriate in the setting of muscle or ligament strain or sprain. This patient does not present following an injury, and sprains of hip joint ligaments (eg, ischiofemoral, iliofemoral, pubofemoral) are less common.</p><p>Abduction bracing (Choice B) and spica cast immobilization (Choice D) are used in the management of developmental dysplasia of the hip, which can present with limited hip range of motion, palpable hip instability, unequal leg lengths, and asymmetric thigh skinfolds.</p><p>Traction (Choice C) is used to pull to length any shortening caused by traumatic fractures of extremity bones. This patient has not sustained any trauma or fractures.</p>",
"21": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Infants are typically able to achieve and maintain a sitting position unsupported by age 9 months. Infants who cannot sit without support at this age demonstrate delayed gross motor development.</p><p>Developmental milestones refer to the actions that most infants or children can perform at a given age. By age of 9 months, infants are typically able to initiate and maintain a sitting position without support. The patient in this example is unable to sit without support, indicating that his gross motor development is delayed. In the fine motor category, infants are expected to be able to rake food toward themselves and move objects between hands. In the language category, most children can make various sounds including \"mama\" and \"dada.\" In the social category, most patients fear strangers, display affect, and look when their name is called. Screening for developmental delays alerts physicians to the potential need for further medical evaluation and/or early referral to developmental specialists. Early intervention has been demonstrated to improve outcomes.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, F, G, and H.</b></p><p>This patient demonstrates normal fine motor (Choices A, B, C, and D), language (Choices A, C, and F), and social (Choices A, C, and G) development. His gross motor development, however, is delayed (Choice H).</p>",
"22": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Primary dysmenorrhea is defined as menstrual pain that accompanies each menstrual cycle but is not associated with any underlying pathology. It typically begins in adolescence. Treatment of primary dysmenorrhea involves the administration of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and topical heat therapy, with combined oral contraceptives and progestin intrauterine device as options for persistent symptoms.</p><p>Primary dysmenorrhea is defined as menstrual pain that accompanies each menstrual cycle but is not associated with any underlying pathology. To diagnose primary dysmenorrhea, causes of secondary dysmenorrhea must be ruled out. These may include endometriosis, leiomyomata uteri (fibroids), adhesions, pelvic inflammatory disease, or adenomyosis. Many of these can be reasonably assessed on pelvic examination, which in this case did not show any abnormalities. Primary dysmenorrhea typically presents with pain in the lower abdomen and pelvis, which may radiate to the lower back or thighs. It is often accompanied by nausea, diarrhea, headaches, and flushing. Symptoms typically begin in adolescence. It can be attributed to uterine vasoconstriction in response to an excess of prostaglandin. Thus, treatment targets this pathway with medications including nonsteroid anti-inflammatory drugs and topical heat therapy. Combined oral contraceptives or a progestin intrauterine device may also be utilized to regulate these symptoms if persistent.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Reassurance (Choice A) would be inappropriate as this patient is having severe symptoms that are likely interfering with her day-to-day activities during her menstrual period. Since there are options for treating this patient's symptoms that have not yet been utilized, oral contraceptive therapy is a more appropriate option.</p><p>Psychiatric assessment (Choice B) is not necessary. Primary dysmenorrhea results from an excess of prostaglandin and is not caused by a psychiatric illness. Suggesting a psychiatric assessment may make this adolescent feel as though the practitioner does not believe her symptoms are real and may harm the patient-physician relationship.</p><p>Narcotic analgesic therapy (Choice C) would not be appropriate at this time. While over-the-counter analgesics have not been effective, oral contraceptive therapy may significantly improve her pain. These should be trialed prior to narcotic therapy, as narcotic therapy is associated with additional side effects and the risk for addiction.</p><p>Laparoscopy (Choice E) can be used to aid in the diagnosis of multiple intra-abdominal pathologies. A common reason for laparoscopy is endometriosis, which presents with chronic dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, dyschezia, and infertility. This patient has none of these symptoms or other intra-abdominal signs or symptoms to justify the need for an invasive procedure like laparoscopy.</p>",
"23": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Mycoplasma pneumoniae is a pleomorphic organism associated with causing atypical pneumonia in young patients. The chest x-ray characteristically has an appearance that is more severe than the patient's clinical presentation, with patchy or diffuse bilateral interstitial infiltrates.</p><p>The diagnosis is most likely pneumonia due to Mycoplasma pneumoniae. Mycoplasma pneumoniae is a pleomorphic organism lacking a cell wall that is typically associated with causing atypical pneumonia in young patients. It is transmitted by respiratory droplets. The most common symptoms include mild fever, headache, and non-productive cough. Physical examination findings may include bilateral crackles and/or wheezing and a vesicular rash. The chest x-ray characteristically has an appearance that is more severe than the patient's clinical presentation, with patchy or diffuse bilateral interstitial infiltrates. Mycoplasma is a fastidious organism and can be grown on Eaton agar, though this can take weeks. It also may not appear on Gram stain because it lacks a cell wall. The diagnosis is usually based on clinical presentation. Laboratory studies that support the diagnosis include a high titer of serum cold agglutinins (IgM antibodies). Treatment is with macrolide antibiotics, doxycycline, or fluoroquinolones. Other causes of atypical pneumonia that may present similarly include respiratory viruses, Chlamydophila pneumoniae, and Legionella infections.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Pneumonia due to respiratory syncytial virus (Choice A) is less common than acute bronchiolitis in children, though disease can progress from the bronchioles to the lung parenchyma. Patients typically present with fever, tachypnea, rhinorrhea, and wheezing. Patients may also display apneic episodes. In general, bronchiolitis is less common beyond infancy.</p><p>Pneumonia due to Streptococcus pneumoniae (Choice B) classically presents with fever, dyspnea, productive cough, and pleuritic chest pain. Bacterial pneumonia is more often lobar and involves a single lobe rather than being widely distributed.</p><p>Congestive heart failure (Choice D) in children presents with shortness of breath, weight gain, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, and peripheral edema. In children, it is most commonly associated with congenital cardiac malformations, arteriovenous fistula, and severe malnutrition. It would be unusual in a previously healthy 10-year-old.</p><p>Tuberculosis (Choice E) presents with subacute fever, weight loss, night sweats, cough, and malaise in primary disease. Latent tuberculosis is commonly asymptomatic. Reactivation presents similarly to primary disease but occurs after a period of latency, typically affecting the lung apices or resulting in miliary disease.</p>",
"24": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Polycystic kidney disease is caused by genetic mutations that result in the development of multiple cysts in the kidney because of structural abnormalities of the renal tubules. Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease is a form of infantile cystic kidney disease that manifests with the development of numerous small cysts that replace the normal kidney parenchyma, resulting in end-stage kidney disease, which typically requires dialysis and/or transplantation at a young age.</p><p>Polycystic kidney disease can be either autosomal recessive or dominant polycystic kidney disease. Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD) results from a mutation in PKHD1 and is a form of infantile cystic kidney disease that manifests with the development of numerous small kidney cysts that replace the normal kidney parenchyma, resulting in end-stage kidney disease, which typically requires dialysis and/or transplantation at a young age. It is associated with hepatic fibrosis and Caroli disease. Aneurysmal vascular disease and hepatic cysts are often present in the autosomal dominant form of polycystic kidney disease. It has a variable age of presentation during fetal life or childhood but can be a potential cause of oligohydramnios and pulmonary hypoplasia (Potter sequence) if it develops in utero.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Nephroblastoma (Wilms tumor) (Choice A) is the most common kidney malignancy in childhood. It occurs secondary to mutations in tumor suppressor genes WT1 or WT2. It is characterized by a large, often palpable, unilateral flank mass and hematuria. It does not typically cross midline and is often smooth to palpation, rather than nodular.</p><p>Neuroblastoma (Choice B) is a malignancy of neuroendocrine cells of the sympathetic nervous system that commonly affects the adrenal glands. It may be differentiated from other common abdominal malignancies of childhood by its tendency to cross the midline and have a nodular texture, along with its association with paraneoplastic opsoclonus-myoclonus syndrome and increased concentrations of serum and urine catecholamine metabolites.</p><p>Renal cell carcinoma (Choice D) is an adenocarcinoma of tubular epithelial cells. Patients may present with gross hematuria (without RBC casts), flank pain, and possible palpable flank mass. Diagnosis of renal cell carcinoma typically occurs with contrast-enhanced CT scan or MRI and is confirmed by biopsy at the time of nephrectomy.</p><p>Renal vein thrombosis (Choice E) occurs most commonly in patients who have an underlying thrombophilic condition and can present with flank pain, hematuria, acute kidney injury, or may be asymptomatic. It would not distort intrarenal architecture like that seen in polycystic kidney disease.</p>",
"25": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A hordeolum, or stye, is a common, acute infection of the oil glands of the eyelid. Patients present with a focal, well-defined subcutaneous nodule at the eyelid margin with associated skin erythema. There may be associated discharge, which may cause ocular surface irritation and conjunctival injection and chemosis. Initial treatment is with warm compresses and gentle massage of the lesion, which help to dissolve inspissated glandular material and promote drainage of the gland.</p><p>A hordeolum, or stye, is a common, acute infection of the oil glands of the eyelid. Patients present with a focal, well-defined subcutaneous nodule at the eyelid margin with associated skin erythema. A blocked glandular orifice may be visible as a focal white point at the eyelid margin with associated discharge, which may cause ocular surface irritation and conjunctival injection and chemosis. A hordeolum may progress to preseptal cellulitis, which is characterized by diffuse erythema, warmth, and edema of the preseptal soft tissues. The underlying cause is obstruction of the oil gland orifice, with subsequent accumulation of sebaceous material and overgrowth of skin flora, most commonly with Staphylococcal species. The meibomian glands or the glands of Zeiss and Moll may be affected. Initial treatment is with warm compresses and gentle massage of the lesion, which help to dissolve inspissated glandular material and promote drainage of the gland. Topical antibiotic ointment is helpful, although systemic antibiotics are not usually necessary. Incision and drainage may be considered for hordeola that do not improve within several weeks. A hordeolum should be distinguished from a chalazion, which is a sterile, noninfectious, chronic, granulomatous inflammation of obstructed meibomian gland material on the eyelid margin.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Nasolacrimal massage (Choice A) is useful for the management of dacryocystitis, or inflammation of the lacrimal sac. Dacryocystitis presents with pain, erythema, and swelling beneath the medial aspect of the lower eyelid. Patients may present with purulent discharge from the lower punctum. Focal swelling at the lid margin, as seen in this patient, is more suggestive of a hordeolum.</p><p>Oral amoxicillin (Choice B) is useful for the treatment of mild preseptal cellulitis but is not typically used in the treatment of hordeolum. Preseptal cellulitis presents with diffuse erythema, warmth, and edema of the preseptal soft tissues, including the eyelid. While preseptal cellulitis may originate from a hordeolum, the absence of diffuse soft tissue erythema suggests against the diagnosis. Preseptal cellulitis must be distinguished from orbital cellulitis, which is more visually threatening, by the absence of pain with extraocular movements, proptosis, visual compromise, and retro-orbital soft tissue enhancement on CT scan.</p><p>Topical prednisolone (Choice C), a glucocorticoid, is useful for reducing intraocular inflammation in the setting of anterior uveitis. It is not helpful for the management of hordeolum and is typically avoided in the setting of suspected infectious conditions.</p><p>Topical tetrahydrozoline (Choice D) is an alpha-2 adrenergic agonist that is available as an over-the-counter eye drop for reducing conjunctival injection. While it may improve the cosmetic appearance of the eye, it does not address the underlying cause of hordeolum and may increase ocular surface irritation.</p>",
"26": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: α1-Antitrypsin deficiency should be suspected in young patients who present with emphysematous lung changes. α1-Antitrypsin is a protease inhibitor responsible for regulating neutrophil elastase. Patients are also at risk for chronic liver inflammation and cirrhosis because of the accumulation of misfolded α1-antitrypsin polymers in hepatocytes.</p><p>The most likely cause of the findings in this patient is α1-antitrypsin deficiency, an inherited protease inhibitor deficiency. α1-Antitrypsin normally regulates neutrophil elastase, which is a serine protease secreted by neutrophils and macrophages as part of a localized inflammatory response. α1-Antitrypsin deficiency is an inherited disorder caused by mutations in the SERPINA1 gene, which encodes α1-antitrypsin. Reduced activity of α1-antitrypsin results in host tissue damage from neutrophil elastase, especially in the lungs. Pulmonary manifestations classically include panlobular emphysema (especially in the lung bases), as suggested by this patient's evaluation. The diagnosis should be suspected in a young patient presenting with evidence of chronic lung disease with bibasilar emphysematous changes. Patients are also at risk for chronic liver inflammation and cirrhosis because of the accumulation of misfolded α1-antitrypsin polymers in hepatocytes.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Congenital lobar emphysema (Choice B) is a rare pulmonary disorder caused by disrupted embryonic bronchopulmonary development and is characterized by air trapping and lung hyperinflation, which can be visualized on chest imaging. The severity of disease varies widely and may be asymptomatic until adulthood or present in the newborn with respiratory distress. α1-Antitrypsin deficiency is more common.</p><p>Kartagener syndrome (Choice C) is an inherited disorder of ciliary function that generally presents in early childhood. Patients typically present with situs inversus, impaired mucociliary clearance with recurrent pneumonia and bronchiectasis, chronic sinusitis, and infertility disorders.</p><p>Pulmonary sequestration (Choice D) is a congenital lung malformation in which a segment of dysplastic, nonfunctional lung tissue is formed that does not communicate with the rest of the airway. Patients typically present with recurrent pneumonia in the same localized region of the lung. Other clinical manifestations include persistent cough, hemoptysis, exertional dyspnea, and back pain.</p><p>Sarcoidosis (Choice E) is a chronic granulomatous inflammatory condition that can affect multiple organ systems, especially the lungs and lymphatic system. It typically presents with a cough, dyspnea, and bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, and potentially with anterior uveitis, erythema nodosum, and arthralgias. Characteristic imaging findings include bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, with or without bilateral upper lobe predominant interstitial infiltrates.</p>",
"27": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: NADPH oxidase is necessary for the formation of reactive oxygen species in phagocytes, which allows for bactericidal activity. Mutations in the proteins that make up the NADPH oxidase complex result in chronic granulomatous disease, typically characterized by recurrent pyogenic bacterial and fungal infections.</p><p>Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD) is a rare primary immunodeficiency syndrome caused by mutations in the gene coding for NADPH oxidase. It is inherited in an X-linked, recessive pattern and results in defective intracellular killing of catalase-positive pathogens because NADPH oxidase is a necessary enzyme in producing the respiratory/oxidative burst. The impaired leukocyte microbicidal activity makes patients susceptible to recurrent pyogenic bacterial and fungal infections with organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus, Staphylococcus epidermidis, Serratia marcescens, Aspergillus species, and Candida species. Symptoms often appear within the first few years of life with recurrent pneumonia, lung abscesses, aspergillosis infection, skin abscesses, osteomyelitis, and chronic diarrhea. Physical examination may show hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy. Diagnosis is made by an abnormal dihydrorhodamine test or a nitroblue tetrazolium reduction test. In the latter test, normal phagocytes use the action of NADPH to reduce nitroblue, which leads to a color change from yellow to blue. Patients with CGD will not demonstrate a color change.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Abnormal leukocyte adhesion (Choice A) and impaired leukocyte chemotaxis (Choice D) are implicated in the pathogenesis of leukocyte adhesion deficiency. It is typically characterized by recurrent bacterial infections, impaired wound healing, a delayed detachment of the umbilical cord after birth, and a complete lack of neutrophils at sites of infections with no pus formation.</p><p>Abnormal leukocyte degranulation (Choice B) is observed in Chédiak-Higashi syndrome. The clinical manifestations include frequent bacterial infections, oculocutaneous albinism, peripheral neuropathy, and progressive neurologic dysfunction. Leukocyte degranulation is normal in chronic granulomatous disease.</p><p>Bone marrow infiltration (Choice C) can occur in leukemia, lymphomas, and myelodysplastic syndromes, resulting in anemia, leukopenia, and/or thrombocytopenia. This patient's clinical picture is more consistent with chronic granulomatous disease.</p>",
"28": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Diabetic ketoacidosis occurs most often in the setting of type 1 diabetes mellitus and may be the initial presentation in an otherwise undiagnosed patient. Patients may present with nausea, vomiting, vague abdominal pain, polyuria, and polydipsia. Laboratory evaluation will show a metabolic acidosis with high anion gap. Treatment includes volume resuscitation and electrolyte management followed by intravenous insulin.</p><p>This patient with nausea, abdominal pain, and a fruity odor to her breath has a presentation consistent with diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). DKA occurs most commonly in the setting of type 1 diabetes mellitus and may be the initial presentation in a patient who has not yet been diagnosed. It is caused by accumulation of serum ketones such as beta-hydroxybutyric acid, acetoacetic acid, and acetone in the setting of insulin deficiency or acute catabolic stressors such as infection. Dysregulated fatty acid degradation and ketone synthesis occurs because of the relative state of starvation created by absent insulin signaling. Patients with DKA may present with fatigue, polyuria, polydipsia, shortness of breath or tachypnea, and diffuse abdominal discomfort. Vital signs may show tachycardia, tachypnea, and hypotension due to severe hypovolemia, which occurs secondary to osmotic diuresis from hyperglycemia. Kussmaul respiration, a rapid and deep breathing pattern, is a classic physical examination finding due to respiratory compensation for the underlying metabolic acidosis. Patients may demonstrate physical examination signs of hypovolemia (eg, dry mucous membranes, decreased skin turgor) along with a sweet breath odor secondary to ketones. Laboratory analysis typically show increased serum glucose and ketones, metabolic acidosis, and a high anion-gap due to the accumulation of unmeasured anions in serum. A metabolic acidosis is accompanied by a decreased bicarbonate concentration in the serum, as bicarbonate is consumed in the neutralization of increased acidic molecules (hydrogen ions) in the blood. The initial management of DKA should focus on volume repletion with isotonic fluids, which is crucial to hemodynamically stabilize the patient, restore tissue perfusion, and clear ketones. Volume repletion should be followed by insulin, correction of electrolyte abnormalities, and treatment of any precipitating conditions.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Exophthalmos (Choice A) is classically seen in association with hyperthyroidism secondary to Graves disease. In addition to exophthalmos, patients with hyperthyroidism may experience weight loss, tachycardia, and tremors. Polyuria, polydipsia, and a fruity odor to the breath would not be expected in a patient with hyperthyroidism.</p><p>Hypertension (Choice B) would not be expected in this patient. Patients with DKA may be hypotensive due to volume depletion in the setting of osmotic diuresis.</p><p>Neutropenia (Choice D) and thrombocytopenia (Choice E) would not be expected in this patient. Patients with DKA may have a normal white blood cell count, or it may be increased due to an underlying infection or demargination. Platelet count can be normal or may be slightly increased as it is an acute phase reactant.</p>",
"29": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cow's milk protein intolerance (CMPI) occurs because of hypersensitivity to antigens in cow's milk and is mediated by IgE-dependent and non–IgE-dependent mechanisms. CMPI presents in infants with colic, regurgitation, diarrhea, hematochezia, urticarial rash, atopic dermatitis, and iron deficiency anemia. Severe cases may rarely present with failure to thrive, wheezing, or anaphylaxis. It is a fundamentally distinct entity from lactose intolerance and resolves in nearly all children by 5 years of age.</p><p>This patient's initial presentation is consistent with cow's milk protein intolerance (CMPI). The underlying cause is a hypersensitivity reaction to unknown non-native antigens in cow's milk and is mediated by both IgE-dependent and non–IgE-dependent mechanisms. CMPI presents in infants with colic, regurgitation, diarrhea, hematochezia, urticarial rash, atopic dermatitis, and iron deficiency anemia. Severe cases may rarely present with failure to thrive, wheezing, or anaphylaxis. The diagnosis is clinical and confirmed with improvement in symptoms following elimination of cow's milk from the diet. In breastfed infants, elimination of cow's milk from the mother's diet is also necessary. In contrast to lactose intolerance, CMPI resolves in half of children by 1 year of age and in nearly all children by 5 years of age.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, and C.</b></p><p>Lifelong lactose intolerance (Choice A) is typical for patients affected by lactose intolerance. Lactose intolerance is a common, often heritable, condition associated with inadequate expression of lactase. It is a distinct clinical entity from CMPI, although it presents similarly in children (and adults) with cramping abdominal pain, bloating, flatulence, and diarrhea. Lactose intolerance typically presents after 2 to 3 years of age, in contrast to CMPI, which presents in infancy. In further contrast to CMPI, lactose intolerance is typically lifelong, while outgrowing lactose intolerance (Choice C) is atypical. Lifelong milk protein intolerance (Choice B) is rare. The great majority of children will outgrow CMPI by 5 years of age.</p>",
"30": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Measles (rubeola) virus is a highly contagious paramyxovirus that causes the disease measles. Measles characteristically presents in children with a prodromal fever along with cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, and a confluent maculopapular rash that starts at the head/neck and spreads to the trunk, excluding the palms and soles. Measles may be prevented with the measles-mumps-rubella vaccine, which is a live attenuated vaccine. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends vaccination with MMR in a two-dose series beginning at age 1 year.</p><p>Measles (rubeola) virus is a highly contagious paramyxovirus that causes the disease measles, which is associated with acute febrile illness and potentially severe sequelae. It is more common in children than adults. It characteristically presents with a prodromal fever along with cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, and a confluent maculopapular rash that starts at the head/neck and spreads to the trunk, excluding the palms and soles. Physical examination may show bright red macules with a bluish-white center on the buccal mucosa (Koplik spots) and lymphadenopathy. Treatment is supportive. Complications of measles include subacute sclerosing panencephalitis, which is uniformly fatal. Measles infection may be prevented with the measles-mumps-rubella (MMR) vaccine, which is a live attenuated vaccine. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends vaccination with MMR in a two-dose series beginning at age 1 year.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, E, G, H, and I.</b></p><p>Diphtheria-tetanus-acellular pertussis (DTaP) (Choice A) vaccine prevents diphtheria, tetanus, and pertussis infections. None of these infections present with a descending maculopapular rash.</p><p>Haemophilus influenzae type b (Choice B) is indicated for all children for the prevention of infection with H. influenzae. H. influenzae can result in various mucosal infections, such as conjunctivitis, otitis media, bronchitis, and pneumonia. It was the leading cause of epiglottitis in children prior to widespread immunization. It does not cause a descending maculopapular rash.</p><p>Hepatitis B (HBV) vaccine (Choice C) prevents viral hepatitis caused by hepatitis B virus and is given at birth and at age 2 months and 6 months. Hepatitis B infection presents acutely with right upper quadrant discomfort, fever, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and increased serum transaminase concentrations.</p><p>Inactivated poliovirus vaccine (IPV) (Choice D) prevents infection with polio virus, which causes poliomyelitis. IPV is given at age 2, 4, 6 to 18 months, and age 4 through 6 years. Global polio vaccination campaigns have eliminated polio from most of the world and it is currently endemic only to Afghanistan and Pakistan.</p><p>Influenza virus (Choice E) causes influenza, which presents with fever, chills, myalgias, malaise, headaches, nausea, and vomiting. It does not generally present with a rash. Annual influenza virus vaccination is recommended for all children beginning at age greater than 6 months.</p><p>Pneumococcal vaccine (Choice G) is indicated for all adults over age 65 years and in patients with high-risk conditions, such as chronic heart, kidney, and liver disease or chronic smokers.</p><p>Typhoid (Choice H) vaccine prevents infection with Salmonella typhi. S. typhi is a gram-negative, encapsulated, facultative anaerobic bacillus that causes typhoid fever, which presents with fever, abdominal pain, rash, and diarrhea.</p><p>Varicella (Choice I) vaccination prevents infection with varicella virus, which causes chickenpox in children and shingles in adults. Chickenpox does not typically present with conjunctivitis or coryza. The rash caused by chickenpox is vesicular and characteristically begins on the trunk, in contrast to the descending, maculopapular rash associated with measles.</p>",
"31": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Trichomoniasis is a sexually transmitted infection causing vaginitis, cervicitis, and urethritis. As Trichomonas vaginalis is sexually transmitted, a young child with trichomoniasis should be evaluated for sexual abuse.</p><p>Trichomoniasis is a sexually transmitted infection by Trichomonas vaginalis that causes vaginitis and cervicitis in women and urethritis in men. Trichomonas commonly presents with vaginal pruritus, dyspareunia, cervical erythema, and abnormal discharge that is green, frothy, and malodorous. Trichomoniasis can lead to overactive bladder (frequency, urgency, urge incontinence). As T. vaginalis is transmitted through sexual contact, a young child with trichomoniasis should be promptly evaluated for sexual abuse.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Urine culture (Choice B) is not needed as the diagnosis of trichomoniasis has already been established by positive T. vaginalis on wet mount. Urine and vaginal cultures are utilized less frequently than nucleic acid amplification testing as it takes days for cultures to result.</p><p>Topical clotrimazole therapy (Choice C) can be utilized to treat candidal vulvovaginitis. It is not a recommended trichomoniasis treatment.</p><p>Intravenous pyelography (Choice D) involves the intravenous injection of radiopaque contrast to subsequently visualize the kidney collecting system, ureters, and bladder under fluoroscopy. This allows for the investigation of suspected urinary tract anatomic abnormalities, malignancies, and strictures. It is not helpful for diagnosing trichomoniasis, and this patient's trichomoniasis diagnosis has already been established.</p><p>Cystoscopy (Choice E), which involves inserting a camera into the urethra and bladder, is typically done in the evaluation of hematuria. It is not helpful for diagnosing trichomoniasis, and this patient's trichomoniasis diagnosis has already been established.</p>",
"32": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Congenital cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection presents with intrauterine growth restriction, microcephaly, intracranial calcifications, hearing loss, seizures, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, thrombocytopenia, and petechial rash. A primary maternal infection has a high risk for vertical transmission via the placenta to the fetus. The diagnosis is confirmed with viral culture or polymerase chain amplification from urine or blood within the first week of life.</p><p>This neonate presents with findings consistent with congenital cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection. CMV, also known as human herpesvirus 5 (HHV-5), belongs to the family of herpesviruses, and possesses a large, double-stranded DNA genome. Congenital CMV infection presents with intrauterine growth restriction, microcephaly, intracranial calcifications, hearing loss, seizures, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, thrombocytopenia, and petechial rash. The diagnosis is confirmed with viral culture or polymerase chain amplification from urine or blood within the first week of life. It is the most common cause of nonhereditary sensorineural hearing loss. In about 10% of cases, hearing loss may be an isolated finding without other symptoms of infection present. Hearing loss is also the most common sequela of an initially asymptomatic neonate. Fetal hydrops is a potential complication of CMV infection, although it is less common than with parvovirus B19 infection. Intracranial calcifications demonstrate a characteristic periventricular distribution and may be detected by ultrasonography or computed tomography. Pregnant women most often contract CMV through exposure from young children, often through daycare centers. A primary maternal infection has a high risk for vertical transmission via the placenta to the fetus. Treatment is with antiviral agents, such as ganciclovir or valganciclovir.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Bone marrow aspiration and analysis (Choice A) is utilized in the diagnosis of lymphoproliferative or lymphodepletive disorders such as lymphoma, leukemia, and aplastic anemia, among others. It does not play a role in the diagnosis of congenital CMV infection.</p><p>Chromosomal microarray (Choice B) is useful for the detection of chromosomal copy number variants, including microdeletions and duplications. It is useful for the diagnosis of children with syndromic abnormalities that are thought to have a genetic cause. It is not useful for the diagnosis of congenital infectious syndromes.</p><p>Lymphocyte enumeration by flow cytometry (Choice C) is useful for the diagnosis of primary immune deficiencies. While infants with immune deficiencies may present with recurrent infections, congenital infections and associated abnormalities suggest an alternate diagnosis.</p><p>MRI of the brain (Choice D) is incorrect. Intracranial calcifications have already been noted on a CT scan in this infant, obviating the need for further cranial imaging at this time. While intracranial findings, such as calcifications or absence of hydrocephalus may be helpful for distinguishing CMV from other congenital infectious syndromes, these findings are not diagnostic.</p><p>Serum rubella IgM titer (Choice E) is incorrect. Congenital rubella infection exhibits a classic triad of cataracts, sensorineural deafness, and congenital heart disease. Neonates also typically display a diffuse purpuric rash.</p><p>Serum Toxoplasma gondii IgM titer (Choice F) is incorrect. Congenital toxoplasmosis presents with the classic triad of chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, and intracranial calcifications, which have a characteristic ring-enhancing appearance. The absence of hydrocephalus and the presence of periventricular calcifications is more consistent with congenital CMV infection.</p>",
"33": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pyloric stenosis is caused by hypertrophy of the pyloric sphincter, leading to gastric outlet obstruction and vomiting. As it commonly presents with recurrent emesis after feedings in male children around 3 to 6 weeks of age, it is associated with hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. Diagnosis is made with abdominal ultrasonography, and treatment requires a surgical pyloromyotomy.</p><p>Pyloric stenosis is caused by hypertrophy of the pyloric sphincter, leading to gastric outlet obstruction and vomiting. It commonly presents with nonbloody, nonbilious emesis after feedings beginning around 3 to 6 weeks of age and is more common in male infants. Symptoms may begin with occasional vomiting, then progress to projectile emesis after every feed, dehydration, and malnutrition as the stenosis worsens. Repeated bouts of vomiting can cause metabolic and electrolyte derangements, classically hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis caused by losses of hydrochloric acid, a major component of stomach secretions. Metabolic alkalosis occurs secondary to dehydration and subsequent volume contraction plus relative excess of bicarbonate. Volume depletion also leads to activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, which in turn stimulates kidney tubule bicarbonate reabsorption and new bicarbonate generation. Metabolic alkalosis, volume depletion, and increased concentrations of aldosterone also lead to renal potassium excretion and hypokalemia. Hypokalemia also contributes to metabolic alkalosis via transcellular exchange of extracellular sodium and protons for potassium ions. Diagnosis of pyloric stenosis is typically by abdominal ultrasonography, which shows thickened pyloric musculature, and treatment requires surgical pyloromyotomy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Choice A demonstrates isolated hypochloremia. Hypochloremia most commonly occurs with metabolic alkalosis, as chloride is excreted in exchange for the reabsorption of bicarbonate in the kidney. This bicarbonate is normal, making this an incorrect profile for a patient with pyloric stenosis.</p><p>Choice C demonstrates a normal basic metabolic panel. This would not be present in a patient with significant vomiting and dehydration.</p><p>Choice D demonstrates an isolated metabolic alkalosis with an increased bicarbonate concentrations but normal electrolytes. As patients with pyloric stenosis lose chloride and potassium through their gastric secretions as well as their kidneys, these patients are more likely to have hypochloremia and hypokalemia associated with their metabolic alkalosis.</p><p>Choice E shows evidence of dehydration with a normal bicarbonate value. This patient has increased values of sodium, potassium, and chloride, suggesting a relative lack of intravascular water and volume depletion. However, patients with pyloric stenosis, despite being dehydrated, have low concentrations of potassium and chloride because of their loss in gastric secretions and through the kidney.</p>",
"34": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Human bites are associated with mixed flora, from both mucosal and skin surfaces. Secondary infection of human bites is common and may present with tenderness, erythema, swelling, purulent drainage, and potentially fever. Patients who suffer human bites should undergo wound irrigation, debridement if necessary, and wounds should be left open due to the high risk for infection. The antibiotic of choice for human bites is amoxicillin-clavulanate acid.</p><p>Human bites are associated with mixed oral flora and skin flora including Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes, Eikenella corrodens, and various anaerobes. Human bites may be accidental, intentional, or the result of a clenched-fist injury. When the skin barrier is interrupted, these injuries are at a high risk for infection, often with multiple organisms. Signs of superficial skin infection include tenderness at the effected site, erythema, swelling, purulent drainage, and potentially fever. Affected patients should be evaluated for the development of infection at the site of injury. In general, these wounds should be irrigated and left open due to the high risk for infection. The first-line antibiotic of choice for infected human bites is amoxicillin-clavulanate acid.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Aerobic gram-positive cocci (Choice A), including Staphylococcus and Streptococcus spp., along with anaerobic gram-positive cocci (Choice B), including Peptostreptococcus are commensal organisms that may be present and associated with additional oral or skin flora in a secondarily infected human bite.</p><p>Gram-negative bacilli (Choice C) include bacteria such as Escherichia coli, Klebsiella spp., and Pseudomonas spp. Although gram-negative bacteria are implicated in many different types of infections, including pneumonia, urinary tract infection and blood stream infections, these bacteria are not commonly associated with secondary infection of human bites.</p><p>Gram-positive bacilli (Choice D) include bacteria such as Clostridium perfringens, which is associated with puncture wound injuries, produces gas gangrene and septic shock that is associated with a high mortality rate. Urgent identification and treatment of gas gangrene with surgical debridement, irrigation, and intravenous antibiotics is required.</p>",
"35": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A history inconsistent with the type of injury present, mechanism of injury inconsistent with child's physical ability, and delay in presentation are all historical factors that should raise suspicion for potential child abuse. If a physician suspects child abuse, child protective services should be notified immediately.</p><p>This infant with a spiral femur fracture after slipping on a wet floor has a presentation concerning for child abuse. There are several \"red flags\" that should alert medical providers to potential child abuse, including a history inconsistent with the type of injury present, mechanism of injury inconsistent with child's physical ability (eg, 3-week-old infant rolled off the bed), and delay in presentation. Certain physical examination findings, such as retinal hemorrhages, bruises on the ears, trunk neck, and certain burn patterns (eg, circumferential hand or foot distribution, cigarette burns) should also alert the provider to potential abuse. Fractures of posterior ribs, metaphyseal corner fractures, and subdural hematomas in an infant are all suspicious imaging findings. Spiral fractures typically occur when a rotational force is applied, such as twisting of a limb. This infant has an injury that is inconsistent with the history that is given, and child protective services (CPS) should be notified immediately.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Measurement of serum calcium concentration (Choice B) may be useful if hypocalcemia was thought to contribute to this patient's fracture. Vitamin D deficiency can lead to hypocalcemia and rickets in children and may increase the risk for fractures. This child does not have any examination findings consistent with rickets.</p><p>Complete blood count (Choice C), bone scan of the right femur (Choice D), and CT scan of the hip (Choice E) are unlikely to be useful for this patient. Furthermore, anytime child abuse is suspected, CPS should be notified right away, and any additional testing can be performed after.</p>",
"36": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Neonatal sepsis is most commonly caused by a bacterial infection and presents with fever or hypothermia, hypoglycemia, lethargy, irritability, and poor feeding. Neonatal sepsis is commonly treated with ampicillin and gentamicin for appropriate broad-spectrum coverage.</p><p>This preterm newborn presents with hypothermia and hypoglycemia, indicating neonatal sepsis. Neonatal sepsis is most commonly caused by a bacterial infection and presents with fever or hypothermia, hypoglycemia, lethargy, irritability, and poor feeding. Evaluation should include a complete blood count, blood culture, urine culture, as well as testing and culture of the cerebrospinal fluid. Common microbiological causes of neonatal sepsis include Escherichia coli, Streptococcus agalactiae (group B), and Listeria monocytogenes. Risk factors for the development of neonatal sepsis include preterm birth, low birth weight, premature rupture of membranes, and maternal infection. The most common pathogen associated with early-onset neonatal sepsis is considered to be Streptococcus (group B), but the frequency of sepsis from this has decreased through the routine use of intrapartum antibiotic therapy for mothers that test positive for it in the antepartum period. Neonatal sepsis is treated with ampicillin and gentamicin for appropriate broad-spectrum coverage.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Ceftriaxone (Choice B) would cover gram-negative rods but would be insufficient to cover Listeria monocytogenes.</p><p>Cilastatin-imipenem (Choice C) is a broad-spectrum antibiotic that treats a variety of organisms. However, ampicillin and gentamicin are sufficient and effective against neonatal sepsis.</p><p>Erythromycin (Choice D) is typically used for topical prophylaxis against bacterial conjunctivitis caused by Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis. Erythromycin is the most used prophylactic agent and functions by binding to the bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit and preventing bacterial protein synthesis. Penicillin G is commonly used to treat syphilis, and tobramycin is an aminoglycoside antibiotic like gentamicin.</p><p>However, penicillin G and tobramycin (Choice E) are not first-line treatment for neonatal sepsis.</p>",
"37": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Treatment for asthma depends on the severity of symptoms but should include low-dose inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) daily, often in combination with an inhaled short-acting β-adrenergic agonist (SABA) as needed. It is no longer recommended to treat asthma with SABA alone as it can lead to overuse and treats only the symptoms rather than the underlying disease. If symptoms persist, other options include inhaled corticosteroids, long-acting β-adrenergic agonist, and oral corticosteroids.</p><p>Asthma, a reversible obstructive lung disease, presents with shortness of breath, cough, tachypnea, increased work of breathing, and wheezing on examination. It is often triggered by exercise, allergens, illness, and smoke. It is characterized by a reversible bronchospasm, which causes obstruction of airflow. Generally, exhalation is affected more than inhalation. The diagnosis is based on clinical history and examination, and confirmed through pulmonary function tests (PFTs), which reveal an obstructive pattern characterized by a decrease in FEV1, decreased FEV1:FVC ratio, and preservation of FVC. Asthma can also be diagnosed based on the reversibility of bronchospasm using beta-agonists or methacholine, with PFTs demonstrating alleviated (beta-agonist) or exacerbated (methacholine) obstruction. Treatment depends on the severity of asthma but initial treatment should include low-dose inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) daily, often in combination with an inhaled short-acting β-adrenergic agonist (SABA) as needed. It is no longer recommended to treat asthma with SABA alone as it can lead to overuse and treats only the symptoms rather than the underlying disease.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Inhaled short-acting β-adrenergic agonist as needed (Choice B) can be included in the initial treatment regimen for patients with asthma, but should not be the only treatment as it can lead to overuse and related risks.</p><p>Intramuscular long-acting corticosteroid every 2 months (Choice C) is not routinely used in the management of asthma.</p><p>Oral corticosteroid daily (Choice D) and oral corticosteroid and oral bronchodilator daily (Choice E) would be considered only if the patient had failed all other treatment options. In addition to a SABA and low-dose inhaled corticosteroid, other options to be considered include medium- and high-dose inhaled corticosteroids, and long-acting β-adrenergic agonists (LABA). If these fail, oral corticosteroids can be considered but should be reserved for severe refractory cases due to their adverse side effect profile when used long term.</p>",
"38": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Ventricular septal defects (VSD) are common congenital heart defects that can cause a left-to-right shunt and present with a holosystolic murmur best heard at the lower left sternal border and a palpable thrill. Persistent VSDs can lead to pulmonary hypertension secondary to volume overload of the right heart and pulmonary vasculature. These increases in right-sided pressures increase the risk for right ventricular hypertrophy, pulmonary hypertension, and right heart failure. Reversal of the shunt can occur with progressive disease.</p><p>Ventricular septal defects (VSD) are among the most common congenital heart malformations. The genetic cause of VSDs is heterogeneous and includes known associations with well-defined syndromes such as thymic aplasia (DiGeorge), Down, or Holt-Oram syndrome. It may also occur in isolation and via nonspecific, random mutations and individual point mutations that are de novo. VSDs are characterized by a holosystolic murmur best heard in the left lower sternal border and may have an associated palpable thrill. VSDs result in a left to right shunt, which increases pulmonary blood flow. This leads to remodeling of the pulmonary vasculature and resultant smooth muscle hypertrophy. In turn, concentric narrowing of the pulmonary arterioles occurs, which results in the development of pulmonary arterial hypertension. If severe and progressive enough, this can cause increased right ventricular pressures and associated myocardial hypertrophy, which can be associated with a narrowly split S2, because of the timing of closing of the pulmonary valve, right ventricular heave, and signs of right heart failure, such as lower extremity edema and hepatomegaly. This patient has evidence of right-sided hypertrophy because of increased pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) in the setting of a long-standing left to right shunt. If this patient's VSD goes uncorrected, the increased right-sided pressures can subsequently lead to reversal of the shunt, presenting with shortness of breath and cyanosis, some of which may already present in this patient who is saturating 93% on room air.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Central venous pressure (Choice A) increase often occurs in the setting of right-sided heart failure or volume overload. This patient's central venous pressure is likely increased given her intracardiac shunt and pulmonary hypertension. However, her physical examination findings are primarily secondary to increased PVR in the setting of a long-standing VSD. An increased central venous pressure would be secondary to her pulmonary hypertension, not the cause.</p><p>Left ventricular end-diastolic pressure (Choice B) is initially decreased in VSDs, as the high-pressure left-sided system can push blood to the low-pressure right-sided system. However, as a patient with a VSD develops pulmonary hypertension, the right-sided pressures increase, preventing movement of blood from left to right. Eventually, this shunt can reverse, causing an increase in left ventricular end-diastolic pressure. All these changes are secondary to changes in PVR.</p><p>Systemic cardiac output (Choice D) changes would not explain this patient's symptoms. Stroke volume from the left heart can be decreased in a patient with VSD because of shunting of blood to the right side of the heart. However, this is compensated for by an increase in heart rate to maintain cardiac output (CO). Additionally, this decrease is minimized by the increased preload to the left heart from the volume-overloaded right heart.</p><p>Systemic vascular resistance (Choice E) increases are not responsible for this patient's condition. Systemic vascular resistance (SVR) may increase in response to a decreased CO or in response to hypoxia to maintain perfusion of distal organs. However, an increased SVR would not cause the right-sided heart failure seen in this patient.</p>",
"39": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hemolytic uremic syndrome may occur as a complication of Escherichia coli O157:H7 infection. E. coli O157:H7 produces Shiga-like toxin that causes hemorrhagic enteritis manifesting as bloody diarrhea, along with microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney failure in hemolytic uremic syndrome. In general, patients are provided supportive care with adequate hydration. Antimotility agents and antibiotics are not recommended.</p><p>This patient's presentation with recent fever and bloody diarrhea and subsequent development of oliguria is suggestive of hemolytic uremic syndrome. Hemolytic uremic syndrome often presents in children and is commonly caused by infection with Escherichia coli O157:H7. E. coli O157:H7, also known as enterohemorrhagic E. coli, produces a Shiga-like toxin that damages the intestinal wall, which initially causes hemorrhagic enteritis and bloody diarrhea. This toxin additionally damages endothelium, which may lead to subsequent microangiopathic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney failure in hemolytic uremic syndrome. Treatment for uncomplicated cases is supportive with oral or intravenous hydration and it is recommended that antimotility and antibiotics are avoided.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Decreased intravascular oncotic pressure (Choice A) is associated with low serum albumin concentration and proteinuria. This often manifests in children as minimal change disease, which is often preceded by upper respiratory infection and associated with proteinuria, edema, and compensatory hyperlipidemia.</p><p>Decreased intravascular volume (Choice B) is associated with dehydration and prerenal azotemia. Prerenal azotemia is associated with an increased blood urea nitrogen:creatinine ratio (over 20:1). In severe cases of volume depletion, hypovolemic shock can result, demonstrated by tachycardia and hypotension.</p><p>Deposition of myoglobin in distal tubules (Choice C) is associated with rhabdomyolysis. Rhabdomyolysis is caused by skeletal muscle breakdown and necrosis, leading to acute kidney injury, release of intracellular contents including potassium, as well as myoglobinuria. In this case, the patient's blood on urinalysis without RBCs is more suggestive of hemoglobinuria as opposed to myoglobinuria.</p><p>Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) (Choice D) is commonly associated with systemic infection. Patients with DIC are often critically ill, have accompanying diffuse purpura, bleeding from catheter sites and mucosal surfaces, and altered mental status. Laboratory abnormalities include anemia, thrombocytopenia, low fibrinogen, and increased D-dimer, prothrombin time, and partial thromboplastin time. DIC patients should be treated for underlying infection and fresh frozen plasma and/or platelets may be needed in the case of life-threatening bleeding.</p>",
"40": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hypoglycemia in the setting of increased insulin concentrations (despite decreased administration) and undetectable C-peptide concentrations is consistent with the administration of exogenous insulin. Hypoglycemia caused by increased endogenous insulin release will be associated with both increased insulin and C-peptide concentrations.</p><p>This patient presents with symptoms of hypoglycemia, which are generally neuroglycopenic (eg, agitation, headache, blurry vision, diplopia, tremor, loss of consciousness), autonomic (eg, diaphoresis, tremoring, nausea, vomiting, cramping, tachycardia, anxiety), and nonspecific (eg, fatigue, weakness, lethargy), often exacerbated by periods of fasting or exercise. Her laboratory evaluation shows increased insulin concentrations and undetectable C-peptide concentrations most likely pointing to the administration of exogenous insulin as the cause of the hypoglycemia. Insulin synthesis occurs in the β-cells of the pancreas. Preproinsulin, synthesized in the rough endoplasmic reticulum, is cleaved into proinsulin, which is stored in cytoplasmic granules. Prior to secretion, proinsulin is cleaved, and insulin and C-peptide are released in equal amounts into the circulation. Hyperinsulinemia due to endogenous secretion is therefore associated with increased C-peptide concentrations, whereas hyperinsulinemia caused by administration of exogenous insulin is not. This patient may have low C-peptide concentrations due to loss of endogenous insulin synthesis, however, her hypoglycemic episodes should have resolved with decreased insulin dosing, and they have not despite a lack of another clear cause of hypoglycemia, raising the likelihood of excess exogenous administration.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Decreased growth hormone production (Choice A) may be associated with fasting hypoglycemia due to increased insulin sensitivity. These patients would be expected to have decreased insulin concentrations in response to hypoglycemia. Children with growth hormone deficiency may also have short stature, increased central adiposity, muscle weakness, and a prominent forehead.</p><p>Increased endogenous insulin production (Choice C) would lead to hypoglycemia with increased C-peptide concentrations.</p><p>Increased epinephrine production (Choice D) leads to increased glycogen phosphorylase activity and a subsequent increase in hepatic glycogen breakdown. This results in hyperglycemia rather than hypoglycemia.</p><p>Insulin resistance (Choice E) would lead to decreased glucose uptake and resultant hyperglycemia.</p>",
"41": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Septic arthritis is a bacterial infection of the joint space, most commonly caused by Staphylococcus aureus. Patients often present with a painful, swollen, erythematous joint with decreased active and passive range of motion. Arthrocentesis is required for diagnosis.</p><p>This patient with an erythematous, swollen, and painful knee with associated decreased range of motion has a presentation consistent with septic arthritis. Septic arthritis results from a bacterial infection of the synovial joint space, most commonly by Staphylococcus aureus, and classically presents with joint pain, worse with loading, ranging, and use, swelling, and overlying erythema. Children, immunocompromised patients, those with a history of traumatic injury to the joint, and those with existing abnormal joint anatomy (eg, rheumatoid arthritis, gout) may be more commonly affected. The mechanism of infection may be direct inoculation, hematogenous spread, or extension from a neighboring local infection (eg, osteomyelitis). Vital signs may show fever and tachycardia. Physical examination classically shows an erythematous, edematous joint with a palpable effusion, and tenderness elicited on range of motion or palpation. If direct trauma preceded the infection, a wound or laceration may be present in association with the joint findings. Laboratory analysis typically shows leukocytosis, along with increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein concentrations. Arthrocentesis is essential to diagnose septic arthritis, and to differentiate the condition from osteoarthritis and inflammatory arthropathies, such as gout and rheumatoid arthritis. Synovial fluid can also be sent for culture and Gram stain plus sensitivity testing of offending organisms, permitting targeted treatment. The leukocyte count in synovial fluid is generally greater than 50,000/mm³ and predominantly neutrophilic when the infectious agent is bacterial, as in this patient. Treatment includes broad spectrum antibiotics, and often arthrocentesis with operative irrigation and debridement is necessary to minimize damage to the joint.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Juvenile idiopathic arthritis (Choice A) is an autoimmune condition characterized by joint pain and inflammation. Patients also commonly exhibit extra-articular manifestations, such as uveitis, lymphadenopathy, and rash.</p><p>Osteomyelitis (Choice B) is an infection of bone and bone marrow. Patients typically complain of pain, difficulty bearing weight, and overlying erythema. This patient's examination is more suggestive of an infection of the joint space rather than of adjacent bone.</p><p>Transient synovitis of the hip (Choice D) and knee (Choice E) would most commonly present after a preceding viral infection. Transient synovitis is an inflammatory condition involving the synovial lining of weight-bearing joints, with the hip being commonly affected. Classic presentation includes pain on weight-bearing, antalgic gait, refusal to ambulate, tenderness over the joint, and pain elicited with range of motion. Vital signs are generally within normal limits as are laboratory studies.</p>",
"42": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Caustic ingestion can lead to significant mucosal damage. Upper endoscopy should be performed 12 to 48 hours after a significant ingestion to assess the extent of damage and further guide management.</p><p>Ingestion of caustic substances may occur accidentally, as is often the case in young children, or intentionally, and can cause significant damage to the esophagus and stomach. Ingestion of strong alkali contained in common household cleaning products and drain cleaner is most common, and results in liquefactive necrosis. Acid ingestion is less common and leads to coagulative necrosis. Clinical presentation varies and depends on the amount and type of substance ingested. Patients may present with oropharyngeal or epigastric pain, dysphagia, odynophagia, and drooling. If the upper respiratory tract is involved, the patient may present with stridor, hoarseness, and difficulty breathing. Early complications include esophageal or gastric perforation, and later complications may include bleeding and formation of fistulae and strictures. Initial management in the emergency department includes respiratory support, fluid resuscitation, and pain control. If there is concern for perforation, chest and abdominal CT scan should be performed immediately, in conjunction with surgical consultation. All patients should have esophagoscopy or upper endoscopy performed 12 to 48 hours after initial ingestion to assess the extent of damage and guide further management.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Administration of sodium bicarbonate solution (Choice A) is unlikely to be helpful, as mucosal injury occurs immediately upon ingestion of the caustic substance. Furthermore, neutralization causes an exothermic reaction that releases heat and may lead to further mucosal damage.</p><p>Barium swallow (Choice B) should be avoided in the early period following caustic ingestion as barium can cause mediastinal inflammation if perforation is present.</p><p>If perforation is suspected, a water-soluble agent such as gastrograffin can be used for evaluation, or CT scan of the chest (Choice C) can be performed. Patients with perforation may present with severe chest or abdominal pain, subcutaneous emphysema, and severe abdominal tenderness with rebound, guarding, and rigidity. This patient with a soft, nondistended and nontender abdomen and normal vital signs is unlikely to have a perforation.</p><p>Placement of a nasogastric tube (Choice E) should be avoided in patients with caustic ingestions, as it may induce retching and vomiting, which can worsen existing mucosal injury and increase risk for esophageal perforation.</p>",
"43": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis are severe mucocutaneous drug reactions with high mortality rates. They are distinguished from each other by the amount of body surface area involvement, &lt;10% for SJS and &gt;30% for TEN, and common causes include beta-lactam antibiotics, anti-epileptics, sulfa-containing medications including trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and allopurinol. Treatment includes identifying and stopping the offending agent, supportive care, and wound management, preferably in a burn intensive care unit.</p><p>Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) is a severe mucocutaneous drug reaction that may progress to toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) with an accompanying high mortality rate. SJS and TEN are distinguished from each other based on the amount of body surface area involvement (&lt;10% for SJS and &gt;30% for TEN) and are driven by the same underlying pathophysiology. Common drugs implicated in the development of SJS or TEN include beta-lactam antibiotics, antiepileptics, sulfa-containing medications including trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and allopurinol. SJS and TEN are characterized by confluent areas of dusky erythema with desquamation of the epidermis. It can be distinguished from a morbilliform drug eruption by the presence of skin pain, desquamation, and mucosal involvement. SJS and TEN are often complicated by infection of the denuded areas of skin, thus meticulous wound care, preferably in a burn unit, is critical. The first step in treatment is to identify and discontinue the offending agents. Supportive care in a burn intensive care unit setting has been shown to improve mortality, but the role of other agents including systemic steroids, cyclosporine, and intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) are controversial.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease) (Choice A) is a multisystemic vasculitic disease in children, characterized by fever for at least 5 days, bilateral nonexudative conjunctivitis, a polymorphous rash, lymphadenopathy, erythema and edema of the hands and feet, and erythema affecting the mucous membranes with dry, cracked lips. Complications include coronary artery aneurysm, and patients are managed with IVIG and aspirin therapy.</p><p>Scarlet fever (Choice B) is associated with group A streptococcus pharyngitis infection and may present with an erythematous tongue, an erythematous rough, papular, sandpaper-like rash, and desquamation of hands and feet.</p><p>Serum sickness (Choice C) is a type III hypersensitivity reaction defined by the presence of circulating antigen-antibody complexes that deposit in tissues. Deposition of these complexes triggers compliment activation and subsequent inflammation. Clinical features include fever, malaise, urticarial rash, and arthralgias or arthritis. Serum sickness occurs after inoculation to a protein antigen from a nonhuman species, such as equine botulism antitoxin, equine anti-thymocyte globulin, or murine monoclonal antibodies (eg, rituximab, infliximab).</p><p>Staphylococcal scalded-skin syndrome (Choice D) occurs due to the release of exfoliative toxin from Staphylococcus aureus infection. It most commonly presents in children and immunosuppressed adults with a diffuse erythematous rash that starts in the skin folds and perioral areas, then progresses to generalized superficial desquamation associated with fevers and malaise.</p>",
"44": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients with Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome typically present with recurrent infections by encapsulated bacteria and opportunistic infections, as well as thrombocytopenia and eczema.</p><p>Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome is caused by a mutation in the WAS gene on the X-chromosome that encodes a protein essential for actin cytoskeleton rearrangement that occurs during interactions between T lymphocytes, antigen-presenting cells, and B lymphocytes, leading to an impaired innate and adaptive immune system. The phenotype is variable, and patients can present with eczema, thrombocytopenia, and infections with encapsulated bacteria and opportunistic pathogens. Encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae can cause recurrent otitis media, pneumonia, and meningitis in patients with Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome. Eczematous rashes may be seen in many genetic disorders including atopic dermatitis, autosomal dominant hyper-IgE syndrome (Job syndrome), and Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Chronic granulomatous disease (Choice A) is caused by NADPH oxidase deficiency and results in impaired intracellular killing of pathogens. This presents as recurrent pyogenic infections and recurrent pneumonia. The most common bacterial infections include Staphylococcus spp, Burkholderia cepacia, and Nocardia spp. Patients with chronic granulomatous disease are also at risk for fungal infections, especially Aspergillus spp. Diagnosis is made by an abnormal dihydrorhodamine test or a nitroblue tetrazolium reduction test.</p><p>Acute HIV infection (Choice B) typically presents with systemic inflammatory symptoms, such as fever, chills, night sweats, fatigue, weight loss, and sore throat.</p><p>Severe combined immunodeficiency (Choice C) can result from numerous mutations but commonly presents with recurrent bacterial, viral, and fungal infections due to severe impairment in both B- and T-lymphocyte function.</p><p>Thymic-parathyroid dysplasia (DiGeorge syndrome) (Choice D) is caused by a 22q11 deletion that leads to failure of the third and fourth branchial pouches to develop. These structures give rise to the thymus and parathyroid glands, and their absence leads to thymic aplasia and hypocalcemia in the setting of cardiac and craniofacial defects. Patients are susceptible to viral and fungal infections. A definitive diagnosis requires fluorescence in-situ hybridization to detect the missing DNA segment.</p>",
"45": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Testicular cancer often presents as a painless, firm nodule in those assigned male at birth, either in adolescence or before age 40 years. Germ cell tumors comprise most testicular neoplasms and laboratory results often reveal increased serum LDH, α-fetoprotein, or β-hCG. Treatment requires orchiectomy with possible retroperitoneal lymph node dissection and chemotherapy depending on the presenting stage of disease.</p><p>Testicular cancer most often occurs in those who were assigned male at birth, either during adolescence or before age 40 years. It presents with a painless, firm nodule in the testis, which is commonly asymptomatic, although it can also present with a dull ache in the surrounding region. Patients may report first noticing the lesion following a testicular trauma. Testicular tumors can be categorized as germ cell tumors and non-germ cell tumors. Germ cell tumors comprise most testicular cancer and include seminoma, choriocarcinoma, teratoma, yolk sac tumor, and embryonal carcinoma. Non-germ cell tumors are often sex cord-gonadal stromal tumors originating from Sertoli or Leydig cells. Risk factors for the development of germ cell tumors include cryptorchidism, family history, seminiferous tubule dysgenesis (Klinefelter syndrome), and heavy marijuana use. Laboratory evaluation will often reveal increased concentrations of LDH, α-fetoprotein, or β-hCG, the last of which can cause gynecomastia, depending on the subtype of testicular cancer. Ultrasonography is the initial step in evaluation of a palpable testicular mass. Malignant neoplasms will show a heterogeneous mass with solid or cystic components, sometimes including calcifications. Initial metastatic spread is commonly to the para-aortic lymph nodes. Treatment requires orchiectomy with possible retroperitoneal lymph node dissection and chemotherapy depending on the presenting stage of disease.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Serum alkaline phosphatase activity (Choice A) is a useful test in the evaluation of biliary disease and bone disease. Measurement of gamma-glutamyl transferase, an enzyme found primarily in the biliary tree, can be used to distinguish the source of increased alkaline phosphatase. Metastatic cancer to either of these organs can cause increased alkaline phosphatase activity on laboratory testing.</p><p>Serum CA 125 concentration (Choice B) is a tumor marker closely associated with ovarian cancer. Concentrations can also be increased in endometrial, fallopian tube, breast, and lung cancer. It has poor specificity and positive predictive value for cancer, as it can be increased by several benign gynecologic processes such as menstruation and pregnancy. Serum CA 125 measurement is therefore most useful as a tool to track cancer treatment response and to monitor for recurrence.</p><p>Serum prostate-specific antigen (PSA) concentration (Choice D) is increased in benign prostatic hyperplasia, prostatitis, and prostate cancer. It can also be increased after manipulation of the prostate by sexual activity, a digital rectal examination, or a procedure. Measurement of PSA for screening of prostate cancer remains controversial, but it remains a useful tool to track cancer treatment response and to monitor for recurrence.</p><p>Urine homovanillic-vanillylmandelic acid (Choice E) concentration is increased in catecholamine-secreting tumors, such as neuroblastoma and pheochromocytoma. The presentation of neuroblastoma is variable depending on the location but can include abdominal pain and distension, weight loss, failure to thrive, and opsoclonus myoclonus syndrome. Pheochromocytoma presents with episodic headaches, sweating, palpitations, tremors, agitation, tachycardia, tachypnea, and treatment-refractory hypertension.</p>",
"46": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Caput succedaneum is characterized by the development of edema and hematoma in the subcutaneous tissues of the supragaleal scalp during or shortly after birth. Its ability to extend across suture lines helps to distinguish it from cephalohematoma, a subperiosteal hemorrhage that is bound by suture lines. Caput succedaneum is usually self-limited and often resolves within 48 hours.</p><p>Caput succedaneum is a benign condition that is caused by peripartum trauma to the infant's scalp. It is characterized by the development of edema and/or a hematoma in the subcutaneous tissues of the scalp above the galea. Because it is above the periosteum, it will cross suture lines. Presentation is typically during delivery or shortly thereafter. Physical exam will demonstrate a boggy or fluctuant area of soft tissue swelling that crosses sutures lines and can cross the midline. Caput succedaneum is usually self-limited and often resolves within 48 hours.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Cephalhematomas (Choice B) are traumatic subperiosteal hematomas that can occur with routine vaginal deliveries or those assisted by vacuum or forceps. They present several hours after birth with firm or fluctuant nontender scalp swelling that does not cross suture lines (limited to one cranial bone). They are usually self-limited and resorb spontaneously within 2 weeks to 3 months but can develop complications, including calcification of the hematoma with prolonged bony swelling and cranial deformities, infection, and osteomyelitis. Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia can occur due to the resorption of extravasated blood products.</p><p>Epidural hematomas (Choice C) classically present following head trauma with initial loss of consciousness followed by a brief period of lucidity before later developing altered mental status, headache, emesis, lethargy, and signs of cerebral compression. A noncontrast CT scan of the head will show an extracerebral lens-shaped hyperdense lesion bound by suture lines, often with compression of adjacent brain or midline shift. They are caused by high-energy trauma, often to the middle meningeal artery, and are not commonly encountered in the peripartum setting.</p><p>Intracerebral hematoma (Choice D) presents acutely with headache, altered mental status, hemiplegia, vision changes, aphasia, or coma depending on the area affected. More common in adults, it is visualized on CT scan as a hyperdensity within the parenchyma. It should not be confused with intraventricular hemorrhage, caused by bleeding from the germinal matrix in preterm and low birth weight in infants.</p><p>Subdural hematoma (Choice E) commonly presents following trauma with a collection of blood beneath the dura caused by shearing of the bridging veins. They appear on CT scan as crescent-shaped fluid collections abutting the internal surface of the skull, not bound by suture lines. The presence of a subdural hematoma in an infant should raise suspicion for nonaccidental trauma, which typically presents with lethargy or altered mental status, seizures, apnea, and retinal hemorrhages.</p>",
"47": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Achondroplasia is the result of mutations in the FGF3 gene, leading to constitutive activation of the gene, and inhibition of endochondral ossification required for normal longitudinal bone development. Failure of long bone growth leads to short stature and is seen in conjunction with macrocephaly, frontal bossing, varus deformity, and hypotonia in infancy. Diagnosis of achondroplasia is based on clinical and radiologic findings.</p><p>Achondroplasia is most commonly associated with mutation in the fibroblast growth factor (FGF) 3 gene. This mutation in the FGF3 gene causes constitutive activation and downstream effects including inhibition of endochondral ossification required for the development of long bones. Patients with achondroplasia present with macrocephaly with frontal bossing and a depressed nasal bridge, short stature secondary to decreased length of the upper and lower limbs, varus deformity, and hypotonia in infancy. Diagnosis is made based on clinical and radiologic findings. Most gene mutations are spontaneous in origin, although patients pass down the mutation in an autosomal dominant pattern.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Cartilage oligometric matrix protein (Choice A) is an extracellular matrix protein present in cartilage and may be used as a marker for cartilage turnover. This gene is not involved in the development of achondroplasia.</p><p>Sulfate transport protein (Choice C) is present in developing and mature cartilage, as well as several other organs and is not associated with the development of achondroplasia.</p><p>Type 1 collagen (Choice D) is found in bones, skin, tendon, and dentin. Defects in type 1 collagen are associated with osteogenesis imperfecta. Osteogenesis imperfecta presents with multiple fractures following minimal trauma in infancy, characteristic blue sclerae, and conductive hearing loss.</p><p>Type 2 collagen (Choice E) is found in cartilage, the vitreous humor and nucleus pulposus. Defects in type 2 collagen have not been associated with the development of achondroplasia.</p>",
"48": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Annual influenza virus vaccination is recommended for the prevention of influenza beginning at age greater than 6 months. Influenza is a seasonal illness, affecting patients typically in winter and early spring months. Patients should be administered influenza vaccine annually in the fall.</p><p>Influenza is a respiratory viral illness caused by influenza virus, an orthomyxovirus that is transmitted via respiratory droplets and causes seasonal pandemics. Symptoms include fever, chills, myalgias, malaise, headaches, nausea, and vomiting. Pneumonia may develop. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends annual influenza virus vaccination beginning at age greater than 6 months. The influenza vaccine is available as a trivalent or quadrivalent intramuscular injection of inactivated virus or as a nasal spray of live attenuated virus. Influenza is a seasonal illness, affecting patients typically in winter and early spring months. Patients should be administered influenza vaccine annually in the fall.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Diphtheria-tetanus-acellular pertussis (DTaP) vaccine (Choice A) is incorrect. DTaP vaccine is given as a three-dose series at ages 2, 4, and 6 months, followed by booster injections between age 15 to 18 months and between age 4 to 6 years.</p><p>Haemophilus influenzae type b vaccine (Choice B) is indicated for all children for prevention of infection with Haemophilus influenzae. H. influenzae can result in various mucosal infections, such as conjunctivitis, otitis media, bronchitis, and pneumonia. It was the leading cause of epiglottitis in children prior to widespread immunization. The vaccine is administered in a two or three-dose series between age 2 and 6 months, followed by a booster at age 12 to 15 months.</p><p>Meningococcal vaccine (Choice D) is indicated for all children and is typically administered prior to age 23 months. An additional serogroup B vaccine is indicated for all children over age 10 years at increased risk for meningococcal disease (eg, dormitory, military living).</p><p>Pneumococcal vaccine (Choice E) is indicated for all adults over age 65 years and in patients with high-risk conditions, such as chronic heart, kidney, and liver disease or chronic smokers.</p>",
"49": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Transverse vaginal septum may occur when there is failure of canalization of the vaginal canal, preventing the excretion of menstrual blood, and presents with primary amenorrhea, cyclic pelvic pain, and a bulging pelvic mass. Diagnosis is made by pelvic ultrasonography. A transverse vaginal septum is treated with transvaginal incision and drainage with resection of the septum and evacuation of the accumulated blood.</p><p>A transverse vaginal septum may occur when there is failure of canalization of the vaginal canal, preventing the excretion of menstrual blood. A transverse vaginal septum presents with primary amenorrhea, cyclic pelvic pain, and a bulging pelvic mass in an adolescent with otherwise normal sexual development. Significant accumulation of menstrual blood products may give the bulging septum a blue appearance that is present on physical examination, a condition known as hematocolpos. Diagnosis is made by pelvic ultrasonography. A transverse vaginal septum is treated with transvaginal incision and drainage with resection of the septum and evacuation of the accumulated blood.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Karyotype analysis (Choice A) is not necessary. While karyotype abnormalities, such as gonadal dysgenesis 45,X (Turner syndrome), can cause amenorrhea, they are often associated with other physical manifestations not present in this patient. In the setting of gonadal dysgenesis 45,X, these include a short stature, webbed neck, and swelling in the hands and feet. This patient has evidence of hematocolpos on examination, making a transverse vaginal septum the most likely diagnosis.</p><p>Urine culture (Choice B) would be useful in the diagnosis of a urinary tract infection (UTI). UTIs most commonly present with dysuria, urinary frequency/urgency, and suprapubic pain. It would not explain this patient's cyclical cramping or hematocolpos on examination.</p><p>Intravenous pyelography (Choice C) is used to evaluate abnormalities of the genitourinary system, including lesions of the kidney, ureters, bladder, and urethra. It is not used in the evaluation of primary amenorrhea. Additionally, this patient has no urinary symptoms to suggest the need for intravenous pyelography.</p><p>Laparoscopy (Choice E) can be used to aid in the diagnosis of multiple intra-abdominal pathologies. A common reason for laparoscopy is endometriosis, which presents with chronic dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, dyschezia, and infertility. This patient exhibits none of these symptoms or other intra-abdominal signs or symptoms to justify the need for an invasive procedure like laparoscopy.</p>",
"50": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Intussusception occurs when a segment of bowel telescopes into an adjacent segment resulting in colicky abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and potentially bloody, mucoid stools. A barium enema is useful in the diagnosis as well as treatment for intussusception.</p><p>Intussusception occurs when a segment of bowel telescopes into an adjacent segment, resulting in colicky abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and potentially bloody, mucoid stools. Classically, it presents in an infant or young child with recurrent episodes of severe abdominal pain often punctuated by asymptomatic periods. Intussusception typically occurs because of the presence of a pathologic lead point, often lymphoid hyperplasia, lymphadenopathy, Meckel diverticulum, stricture, or malignancy, which acts to draw one segment of bowel into an adjacent segment during peristalsis. If the intussusception self-resolves, there may be no symptoms. If it does not resolve, traction on mesenteric vessels can result in hypoperfusion of the segment with resultant inflammation, hemorrhage, obstruction, and pain. It may be detected on physical examination as an elongated mass, often in the right upper quadrant. Diagnosis consists of clinical history and physical examination and is supported by imaging. Ultrasound evaluation is preferred in young children and will show telescoping interposed bowel segments in longitudinal orientation, or a targetoid appearance in transverse orientation. A contrast enema can also show intussusception with appearance of a mass prolapsing into the lumen and an inability of the contrast to reach the proximal bowel. Treatment of an ileocolic intussusception requires the administration of a contrast enema, which reduces the intussusception. Contrast enema is both diagnostic and therapeutic in this setting. Surgery may be required in cases where there is repetitive failure of the enema to reduce the intussusception or if the patient has symptoms concerning for bowel necrosis or perforation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Appendicitis (Choice A) typically presents with loss of appetite, nausea, vomiting, and right lower quadrant pain. It is often associated with fever and leukocytosis. It does not typically cause bloody stools.</p><p>Dysentery (Choice B) is characterized by bloody diarrhea, typically caused by bacteria such as Shigella and enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli. This patient has no risk factors for bacterial diarrhea. Intermittent episodes of abdominal pain and vomiting in a young child is more suspicious for intussusception.</p><p>Viral gastroenteritis (Choice D) is a common, typically self-limiting illness in children characterized by nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and mild abdominal discomfort. It would usually not cause bloody stools.</p><p>Midgut volvulus (Choice E) typically occurs in the setting of malrotation secondary to the incomplete rotation of bowel during embryonic development. This presents with bilious vomiting, abdominal distension, and pain, and when severe, intestinal necrosis, hematochezia, perforation, and peritonitis.</p>"
};
